00:00:32.930 --> 00:00:35.180
GUEST SPEAKER 1: And a lot
of the women in this field,

00:00:35.180 --> 00:00:39.050
about 20 of them, are
part of this co-op called

00:00:39.050 --> 00:00:40.850
the Solar Women of Totogalpa.

00:00:40.850 --> 00:00:43.640
And these women,
basically, they get

00:00:43.640 --> 00:00:45.230
grants from different
funding sources

00:00:45.230 --> 00:00:48.445
to research sustainable
energy options.

00:00:48.445 --> 00:00:49.820
So one of the
things that they do

00:00:49.820 --> 00:00:51.320
is they make their
own solar panels.

00:00:51.320 --> 00:00:53.392
And the people in
the village can

00:00:53.392 --> 00:00:55.100
buy these either with
money or with hours

00:00:55.100 --> 00:00:57.920
spent working in the co-op.

00:00:57.920 --> 00:01:03.575
So we decided that even though
they're making 60-watt panels--

00:01:03.575 --> 00:01:05.450
does not match that--
but even though they're

00:01:05.450 --> 00:01:07.730
making 60-watt
panels like these,

00:01:07.730 --> 00:01:09.890
they also all have cell
phones, but a lot of them

00:01:09.890 --> 00:01:10.850
are off the grid.

00:01:10.850 --> 00:01:12.570
So they also need ways
of charging these,

00:01:12.570 --> 00:01:15.600
and we thought that solar power
was a good way of doing this.

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:20.300
So what they're doing right
now to make these is-- this

00:01:20.300 --> 00:01:21.050
is a poor image.

00:01:21.050 --> 00:01:24.260
But basically, they take
the smaller cells, which

00:01:24.260 --> 00:01:27.510
are 8 by 15 centimeters,
and these are rejects,

00:01:27.510 --> 00:01:31.068
so they're not as
efficient as standard cells

00:01:31.068 --> 00:01:33.110
that we might use here,
but they're much cheaper,

00:01:33.110 --> 00:01:34.003
so they use those.

00:01:34.003 --> 00:01:35.670
They might hand solder
them all together

00:01:35.670 --> 00:01:38.480
and line them up, put
liquid silicone over them,

00:01:38.480 --> 00:01:41.795
and then put a glass piece over
it, and then encapsulate it all

00:01:41.795 --> 00:01:43.670
and make it look much
nicer like the ones you

00:01:43.670 --> 00:01:45.840
saw in the previous slide.

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:49.190
So this is a much
smaller, much nicer

00:01:49.190 --> 00:01:51.770
looking one that is made
in China and imported,

00:01:51.770 --> 00:01:54.140
but it's available
for $20 in Managua,

00:01:54.140 --> 00:01:57.260
which is about three hours
from the town we stayed in.

00:01:57.260 --> 00:01:59.370
And so these are very
difficult to get,

00:01:59.370 --> 00:02:01.793
and they're pretty
expensive, but they also

00:02:01.793 --> 00:02:03.210
would like to be
able to make them

00:02:03.210 --> 00:02:05.600
there so that it's
about the same price,

00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:08.210
but it's more accessible.

00:02:08.210 --> 00:02:12.480
It employs some people, and
it's just more sustainable.

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:16.085
So that's what we set out to do.

00:02:16.085 --> 00:02:18.540
GUEST SPEAKER 2: So
as was mentioned,

00:02:18.540 --> 00:02:21.350
they do have access
to solar cells,

00:02:21.350 --> 00:02:23.240
but unfortunately,
what happens right now

00:02:23.240 --> 00:02:25.250
is to make the
cell phone charger,

00:02:25.250 --> 00:02:26.662
they need to cut these cells.

00:02:26.662 --> 00:02:29.120
And what they do is, basically,
they cut one of these cells

00:02:29.120 --> 00:02:31.320
into four parts and then
attach them in series

00:02:31.320 --> 00:02:35.120
so that the voltages add up,
so you can get up to 6 volts.

00:02:35.120 --> 00:02:37.160
Unfortunately,
with this process,

00:02:37.160 --> 00:02:39.740
what ends up happening is you
end up using a lot of the cells

00:02:39.740 --> 00:02:42.980
just because the method
that they use to cut them

00:02:42.980 --> 00:02:46.827
is not foolproof, and so you end
up cracking a lot of the cells.

00:02:46.827 --> 00:02:48.410
And when you talk
to them, we realized

00:02:48.410 --> 00:02:51.180
that they waste about
half the cells they cut,

00:02:51.180 --> 00:02:54.140
which means that if they
needed to use 9 cells,

00:02:54.140 --> 00:02:57.020
they'd end up using 18
because they'd waste half.

00:02:57.020 --> 00:02:59.960
And so we figured that if
we could somehow step up

00:02:59.960 --> 00:03:02.330
the voltage given by
three original cells,

00:03:02.330 --> 00:03:04.702
they called these original
cells, if you could step up

00:03:04.702 --> 00:03:06.410
the voltage given by
three original cells

00:03:06.410 --> 00:03:08.600
to the required 5
volts to charge a cell

00:03:08.600 --> 00:03:10.700
phone without cutting
them, it would save them

00:03:10.700 --> 00:03:12.690
a lot of time and money.

00:03:12.690 --> 00:03:16.040
And so what we decided to do
was connect three of these cells

00:03:16.040 --> 00:03:18.650
in series, so you get
a 1.5-volt output.

00:03:18.650 --> 00:03:22.550
And then have that output run
through your circuit, which

00:03:22.550 --> 00:03:23.960
steps it up to 5 volts.

00:03:23.960 --> 00:03:27.710
Now, the circuit
basically works like this.

00:03:27.710 --> 00:03:29.480
So this is your input.

00:03:29.480 --> 00:03:33.380
You have current inputting
data at 1.5 volts.

00:03:33.380 --> 00:03:34.880
It goes through
your op amp and then

00:03:34.880 --> 00:03:40.700
steps it up to 1.25
or 3.3, depending

00:03:40.700 --> 00:03:42.410
on how it's connected.

00:03:42.410 --> 00:03:46.050
For our purposes, we needed 5,
so we connected it accordingly.

00:03:46.050 --> 00:03:48.513
These are capacitors, an
inductor, and a zener diode.

00:03:48.513 --> 00:03:50.930
The zener diode is used to
make sure that the voltage goes

00:03:50.930 --> 00:03:53.460
in one direction only.

00:03:53.460 --> 00:03:55.460
And so basically,
these two leads

00:03:55.460 --> 00:03:58.580
have a voltage difference
of 5 volts across them

00:03:58.580 --> 00:04:02.750
when connected to the solar
panels and up in the lighting.

00:04:02.750 --> 00:04:04.520
And what we did was, we then--

00:04:04.520 --> 00:04:08.570
so this is we surveyed what
kind of cell phones are these

00:04:08.570 --> 00:04:11.960
and realized that, for the
most part, they're Nokias,

00:04:11.960 --> 00:04:14.870
and this is the most common
type of phone that's available

00:04:14.870 --> 00:04:16.110
there.

00:04:16.110 --> 00:04:20.360
And we got the adapter that
they used to charge this,

00:04:20.360 --> 00:04:23.780
cut out the phone,
and attached it here.

00:04:23.780 --> 00:04:31.770
If you look at our
prototype, you notice that.

00:04:31.770 --> 00:04:36.450
So the orange and black
wires are these, basically,

00:04:36.450 --> 00:04:40.880
and then they
connect to your port.

00:04:40.880 --> 00:04:46.580
And then basically, the idea
is that you plug it in, put it

00:04:46.580 --> 00:04:51.030
on sunlight, and
use this as a way

00:04:51.030 --> 00:04:55.860
to make sure that you're
always facing the sun openly.

00:04:58.530 --> 00:05:01.530
But there are a lot of
notches along the sides.

00:05:01.530 --> 00:05:04.290
And so basically,
you wedge this in,

00:05:04.290 --> 00:05:07.500
and then you can change
the angle of your panel.

00:05:12.440 --> 00:05:15.590
So this is just a very
rudimentary cost breakdown

00:05:15.590 --> 00:05:22.430
of what we estimate our cell
phone charger to cost them.

00:05:22.430 --> 00:05:28.010
Basically, I mean, finally,
this is the number that we came.

00:05:28.010 --> 00:05:31.790
We were trying to shoot for $20,
and we're slightly above that.

00:05:31.790 --> 00:05:36.440
But we figure that there's
two ways to optimize costs,

00:05:36.440 --> 00:05:39.925
and that is very viable for
them to get this for $20.

00:05:42.900 --> 00:05:45.650
Yeah, basically, this is
just to give you an idea

00:05:45.650 --> 00:05:47.390
how expensive the
components are.

00:05:47.390 --> 00:05:49.723
I don't want to run through
each one of them just like--

00:05:49.723 --> 00:05:51.515
GUEST SPEAKER 3: I'm
sorry, just to mention

00:05:51.515 --> 00:05:52.550
a really quick thing.

00:05:52.550 --> 00:05:56.200
We're also including
$4 for labor,

00:05:56.200 --> 00:05:59.300
so even though it
is produced locally

00:05:59.300 --> 00:06:04.297
and it is providing employment,
whereas the internet,

00:06:04.297 --> 00:06:06.380
the China made one is not
providing any employment

00:06:06.380 --> 00:06:06.935
for the community.

00:06:06.935 --> 00:06:07.490
GUEST SPEAKER 2: Right.

00:06:07.490 --> 00:06:08.060
Exactly.

00:06:08.060 --> 00:06:11.750
China made one cell
for $80, but they

00:06:11.750 --> 00:06:13.400
don't provide any
source of income

00:06:13.400 --> 00:06:16.790
to the people in Managua.

00:06:16.790 --> 00:06:24.380
And so this is just to give you
an idea of the cost breakdown.

00:06:24.380 --> 00:06:28.610
This is just a
blown-up image of us.

00:06:28.610 --> 00:06:31.940
Again, just to give you an idea
of the components that we used.

00:06:31.940 --> 00:06:38.240
Essentially, this circuit is
enclosed by this casing right

00:06:38.240 --> 00:06:38.850
here.

00:06:38.850 --> 00:06:42.710
So it's that circuit
basically, underneath this.

00:06:42.710 --> 00:06:44.900
GUEST SPEAKER 1: Which
you can see here.

00:06:44.900 --> 00:06:45.530
GUEST SPEAKER 2:
Which you can see

00:06:45.530 --> 00:06:47.630
that in that image, the
next image right there.

00:06:50.300 --> 00:06:53.150
So I mean, like I said,
this is your output

00:06:53.150 --> 00:06:57.290
from the solar cells,
which go into the circuit

00:06:57.290 --> 00:07:01.460
and then go and
charge the cell phone.

00:07:01.460 --> 00:07:04.460
And the actual
circuitry, so this

00:07:04.460 --> 00:07:08.038
to talking about why we
made circuits like this.

00:07:08.038 --> 00:07:09.830
We decided to use a
breadboard because when

00:07:09.830 --> 00:07:13.810
you talk to Mario, who's
our community funder,

00:07:13.810 --> 00:07:16.700
is one of the
community partners,

00:07:16.700 --> 00:07:19.260
he told us that they do
have access to breadboards,

00:07:19.260 --> 00:07:22.010
but don't have access to proto
boards, which we were initially

00:07:22.010 --> 00:07:23.520
fine on using.

00:07:23.520 --> 00:07:26.630
And so we decided to stick
with the breadboard, the model.

00:07:26.630 --> 00:07:29.990
And we also, we figured
the [INAUDIBLE],,

00:07:29.990 --> 00:07:32.330
the various components,
and they are available.

00:07:32.330 --> 00:07:35.430
The only component
that we potentially

00:07:35.430 --> 00:07:38.720
see having to be
shipped there is this.

00:07:38.720 --> 00:07:41.808
But since they already
ship solar cells,

00:07:41.808 --> 00:07:43.850
it would just be another
added component to ship,

00:07:43.850 --> 00:07:45.470
and it's not very
good quality, so we

00:07:45.470 --> 00:07:48.170
think it's definitely viable.

00:07:48.170 --> 00:07:53.120
So that's why we decided to
go with the breadboard design.

00:07:53.120 --> 00:07:56.703
Apart from that-- do you
want to talk about this?

00:07:56.703 --> 00:07:58.370
GUEST SPEAKER 3: But
before that, I just

00:07:58.370 --> 00:08:01.115
wanted to just speak a little
bit about the [INAUDIBLE],,

00:08:01.115 --> 00:08:03.902
in general, why we chose
to build it the way we did.

00:08:03.902 --> 00:08:05.360
First of all, it's
made out of wood

00:08:05.360 --> 00:08:09.320
because they're really
locally-found material.

00:08:09.320 --> 00:08:12.660
So first of all, I'll talk
about the solar panel.

00:08:12.660 --> 00:08:14.480
So this is a solar panel.

00:08:14.480 --> 00:08:16.670
Because we didn't have,
I guess, easy access

00:08:16.670 --> 00:08:19.610
to the liquid silicone and
aluminum and plastics to use,

00:08:19.610 --> 00:08:23.600
we still use acrylic
and a different method

00:08:23.600 --> 00:08:25.960
to glue the cells.

00:08:25.960 --> 00:08:29.060
In Nicaragua, they
make these differently,

00:08:29.060 --> 00:08:31.080
and they customize it
for every application.

00:08:31.080 --> 00:08:33.080
And so when we were talking
to him on the phone,

00:08:33.080 --> 00:08:35.455
he already had to made some
changes to the initial design

00:08:35.455 --> 00:08:38.750
and we had made, and he said
he would change the hinges

00:08:38.750 --> 00:08:39.929
and everything.

00:08:39.929 --> 00:08:42.700
And so it would be
made out of aluminum.

00:08:42.700 --> 00:08:48.260
And so originally, we
wanted to make leg stands

00:08:48.260 --> 00:08:51.440
so we could change the
angle and not just here.

00:08:51.440 --> 00:08:54.950
But then we realized
that it might be easier

00:08:54.950 --> 00:08:58.490
to put something in here
instead of having to have legs.

00:08:58.490 --> 00:09:02.930
So we just came up with the
notches and cut a PCV pipe.

00:09:02.930 --> 00:09:04.790
And then you can
change the angle.

00:09:04.790 --> 00:09:07.350
Moreover, we wanted to create
a way to protect the circuit,

00:09:07.350 --> 00:09:11.570
so we just encased it
around this aluminum frame

00:09:11.570 --> 00:09:14.063
with the two wires hanging out.

00:09:14.063 --> 00:09:15.980
Additionally, these are
really easy to switch,

00:09:15.980 --> 00:09:17.522
so if you do have
another cell phone,

00:09:17.522 --> 00:09:21.500
you could switch the charger
into some other cell phone.

00:09:21.500 --> 00:09:26.150
And another reason why
this is over here, just

00:09:26.150 --> 00:09:27.315
in case of rain.

00:09:27.315 --> 00:09:28.940
I mean, hopefully,
you have it outside,

00:09:28.940 --> 00:09:30.690
and you'll be looking at us.

00:09:30.690 --> 00:09:32.883
Because it rains,
this is waterproof,

00:09:32.883 --> 00:09:34.550
and it will also
protects the cell phone

00:09:34.550 --> 00:09:37.508
and the charger for at
least enough time for you

00:09:37.508 --> 00:09:38.300
to come and get it.

00:09:38.300 --> 00:09:41.960
If it's raining really hard and
breaks, it's going to get wet.

00:09:41.960 --> 00:09:44.632
For the long time, we realized
it really should be protected.

00:09:44.632 --> 00:09:45.590
GUEST SPEAKER 2: Right.

00:09:45.590 --> 00:09:49.730
And like Juan
said, we could also

00:09:49.730 --> 00:09:52.010
have another adapter going
out from the same leads.

00:09:52.010 --> 00:09:54.680
And we actually do
have a USB adapter

00:09:54.680 --> 00:09:57.830
that runs at the same
voltage that we did test.

00:09:57.830 --> 00:09:59.690
But for the purpose
of the demonstration,

00:09:59.690 --> 00:10:02.870
we decided to go with the Nokia
just because it's the more

00:10:02.870 --> 00:10:04.910
locally available phone.

00:10:04.910 --> 00:10:07.700
But we could potentially have
a number of different adapters

00:10:07.700 --> 00:10:08.455
shooting out here.

00:10:08.455 --> 00:10:09.830
It's all run on
the same voltage.

00:10:15.562 --> 00:10:18.780
GUEST SPEAKER 3: So we're doing
some testing in the past couple

00:10:18.780 --> 00:10:21.240
of days to see how much
power we could get out

00:10:21.240 --> 00:10:24.120
of the cell phone, using
the lamp over there,

00:10:24.120 --> 00:10:25.270
you can test it.

00:10:25.270 --> 00:10:27.690
Make sure you have a
demo card, actually,

00:10:27.690 --> 00:10:29.740
at work because it does work.

00:10:29.740 --> 00:10:31.490
GUEST SPEAKER 2: Do
you want to actually--

00:10:31.490 --> 00:10:32.990
I don't think it's
going to stretch.

00:10:32.990 --> 00:10:35.675
Do you want to just get this?

00:10:35.675 --> 00:10:39.963
GUEST SPEAKER 3: So
here is the solar panel.

00:10:39.963 --> 00:10:41.380
GUEST SPEAKER 2:
That's all right.

00:10:41.380 --> 00:10:44.260
GUEST SPEAKER 3: Can make
it incident to the light.

00:10:49.410 --> 00:10:55.770
And you connect it.

00:10:55.770 --> 00:10:57.810
They're charging?

00:10:57.810 --> 00:10:59.933
So it does work?

00:10:59.933 --> 00:11:01.350
You get scared for
a second there.

00:11:01.350 --> 00:11:03.642
GUEST SPEAKER 1: Well, there's
no way to stay charging.

00:11:03.642 --> 00:11:06.035
It just has a little
charging symbol.

00:11:06.035 --> 00:11:07.660
GUEST SPEAKER 3:
Yeah, so it does work.

00:11:07.660 --> 00:11:09.900
However, we wanted to
see how efficient it is

00:11:09.900 --> 00:11:12.750
and how fast we could
get it to actually work.

00:11:12.750 --> 00:11:18.690
We realized that Boston-made
sun is not the same as Nicaragua

00:11:18.690 --> 00:11:20.310
sun on the equator.

00:11:20.310 --> 00:11:24.720
So we conducted-- there
are different lights

00:11:24.720 --> 00:11:27.100
and light outputs.

00:11:27.100 --> 00:11:32.220
This is the output power of
the light of the solar panels

00:11:32.220 --> 00:11:34.710
calculated by just measuring
the current and the voltage

00:11:34.710 --> 00:11:36.967
at the same time
as the solar panel.

00:11:36.967 --> 00:11:38.175
GUEST SPEAKER 1: [INAUDIBLE].

00:11:38.175 --> 00:11:39.630
GUEST SPEAKER 3: And
the output power,

00:11:39.630 --> 00:11:40.890
it's output power
are at least two

00:11:40.890 --> 00:11:43.307
leads, calculated by measuring
the current and the voltage

00:11:43.307 --> 00:11:45.120
at the same time.

00:11:45.120 --> 00:11:51.690
So we are getting this.

00:11:55.080 --> 00:11:57.930
So we then make a standard
for the efficiency,

00:11:57.930 --> 00:12:01.200
and this is for the past
percent of the power

00:12:01.200 --> 00:12:02.730
we're getting right now.

00:12:02.730 --> 00:12:04.960
If we estimate for
Nicaraguan sun,

00:12:04.960 --> 00:12:07.620
we would be getting
around 65% efficiency.

00:12:07.620 --> 00:12:11.400
Right now, we're getting
about 0.2 amps in Boston.

00:12:11.400 --> 00:12:13.740
I mean, 200--

00:12:13.740 --> 00:12:14.700
GUEST SPEAKER 1: No.

00:12:14.700 --> 00:12:18.485
GUEST SPEAKER 3: 0.02 amps,
20 milliamps in Boston.

00:12:18.485 --> 00:12:20.235
if we extrapolated for
how we were getting

00:12:20.235 --> 00:12:22.800
in Nicaragua with the
power in Nicaragua,

00:12:22.800 --> 00:12:23.820
we'd be getting around--

00:12:23.820 --> 00:12:25.230
GUEST SPEAKER 1: 80 to 100.

00:12:25.230 --> 00:12:28.120
GUEST SPEAKER 3: --80
to 100 milliamps,

00:12:28.120 --> 00:12:30.798
which in the cell phone
batteries are about 800--

00:12:30.798 --> 00:12:32.340
GUEST SPEAKER 1:
Yeah, 800 milliamps.

00:12:32.340 --> 00:12:33.632
GUEST SPEAKER 3: 800 milliamps.

00:12:33.632 --> 00:12:37.050
So it would take about 10 hours
to charge the cell phone, which

00:12:37.050 --> 00:12:39.090
is not ideal because
we were aiming for,

00:12:39.090 --> 00:12:40.617
at the beginning was--

00:12:40.617 --> 00:12:41.700
GUEST SPEAKER 1: Five-ish.

00:12:41.700 --> 00:12:43.367
GUEST SPEAKER 3: Four
hours, five hours.

00:12:43.367 --> 00:12:44.610
We're expecting 200.

00:12:44.610 --> 00:12:46.840
The circuit said that
200 milliamps would

00:12:46.840 --> 00:12:48.390
be outputted from the circuit.

00:12:48.390 --> 00:12:51.510
But instead, we're
getting 9 to 100.

00:12:51.510 --> 00:12:54.040
So it's not as efficient
as we would like,

00:12:54.040 --> 00:12:56.612
but it still worked.

00:12:56.612 --> 00:12:57.570
GUEST SPEAKER 2: Right.

00:12:57.570 --> 00:13:01.650
And just to explain why it's
so scattered at a point,

00:13:01.650 --> 00:13:04.800
actually, above the 100%
efficiency mark, which

00:13:04.800 --> 00:13:08.800
is theoretically possible, but
there was probably something

00:13:08.800 --> 00:13:09.600
ahead of--

00:13:09.600 --> 00:13:10.050
GUEST SPEAKER 1:
One of the times

00:13:10.050 --> 00:13:11.370
we were using the actual sun.

00:13:11.370 --> 00:13:15.616
And so it probably changed
a little bit in between.

00:13:15.616 --> 00:13:16.858
GUEST SPEAKER 3: [INAUDIBLE].

00:13:19.730 --> 00:13:20.247
That's it.

00:13:20.247 --> 00:13:21.080
Thank you very much.

00:13:21.080 --> 00:13:22.205
GUEST SPEAKER 2: Thank you.

00:13:22.205 --> 00:13:23.796
Do you guys have any questions?

00:13:23.796 --> 00:13:28.868
AUDIENCE: So how did you
estimate the Nicaraguan sun?

00:13:28.868 --> 00:13:30.410
GUEST SPEAKER 1: We
took measurements

00:13:30.410 --> 00:13:33.500
while we were there as far as
what voltage and current output

00:13:33.500 --> 00:13:36.675
each cell was at
around 1 o'clock.

00:13:36.675 --> 00:13:41.180
GUEST SPEAKER 2: [INAUDIBLE]
based on what we think,

00:13:41.180 --> 00:13:45.371
how much sun that
would relate it.

00:13:45.371 --> 00:13:51.380
AUDIENCE: With
the 10-hour issue,

00:13:51.380 --> 00:13:54.120
the only thing I think of is
the sun moves in 10 hours,

00:13:54.120 --> 00:13:56.650
so the user would have to
understand that they would have

00:13:56.650 --> 00:13:58.180
to move the--

00:13:58.180 --> 00:14:00.505
so it might actually be
longer than 10 hours.

00:14:00.505 --> 00:14:02.077
An optimal sun is 10 hours.

00:14:02.077 --> 00:14:03.160
So it might be more like--

00:14:03.160 --> 00:14:04.460
GUEST SPEAKER 1: The
one thing we noticed

00:14:04.460 --> 00:14:06.370
was when talking to
people, they usually

00:14:06.370 --> 00:14:08.770
didn't actually charge it
from completely discharged

00:14:08.770 --> 00:14:09.340
to charged.

00:14:09.340 --> 00:14:11.140
They usually charge
from about half charge,

00:14:11.140 --> 00:14:12.430
so it would be more
like five hours, which

00:14:12.430 --> 00:14:13.120
is more reasonable.

00:14:13.120 --> 00:14:14.495
Because that's
more like the band

00:14:14.495 --> 00:14:17.065
that sun is usable in a day.

00:14:17.065 --> 00:14:20.400
AUDIENCE: How long is the
cell phone value life?

00:14:20.400 --> 00:14:22.700
GUEST SPEAKER 1: We got
mixed answers on that.

00:14:22.700 --> 00:14:25.250
We did about 10 to 15
interviews of people

00:14:25.250 --> 00:14:27.980
and ask that question, and most
people, I think, on average

00:14:27.980 --> 00:14:29.840
said they charged it
about once a week.

00:14:29.840 --> 00:14:31.940
There was one person who
said once every 15 days,

00:14:31.940 --> 00:14:33.573
one said every three days.

00:14:33.573 --> 00:14:35.240
It just depends on
how much they use it.

00:14:35.240 --> 00:14:36.110
GUEST SPEAKER 2:
It also, I think,

00:14:36.110 --> 00:14:38.690
depends on how many cell
phones they have in the family.

00:14:38.690 --> 00:14:41.510
Because we noticed that
some family had one cell

00:14:41.510 --> 00:14:43.250
phone for the entire
family, so it,

00:14:43.250 --> 00:14:44.720
obviously, drained out faster.

00:14:44.720 --> 00:14:46.970
But if you have
individual cell phones,

00:14:46.970 --> 00:14:49.940
they tend to last longer
because you don't need--

00:14:49.940 --> 00:14:52.140
and so it really depends.

00:14:52.140 --> 00:14:54.140
But for the most
part, like we said,

00:14:54.140 --> 00:14:58.970
I feel once a week was a
very reasonable-- maybe twice

00:14:58.970 --> 00:15:00.620
a week, I guess, on average.

00:15:00.620 --> 00:15:02.193
Not if they don't use it a lot.

00:15:02.193 --> 00:15:03.860
GUEST SPEAKER 1: And
most of them just--

00:15:03.860 --> 00:15:06.003
we noticed just from
living with the families,

00:15:06.003 --> 00:15:08.420
a lot of the times, the cell
phone just stays at the house

00:15:08.420 --> 00:15:09.712
so that if they receive a call.

00:15:09.712 --> 00:15:11.750
So they can actually
still be using it

00:15:11.750 --> 00:15:13.230
while it's charged
because they'll

00:15:13.230 --> 00:15:14.230
be within hearing shots.

00:15:14.230 --> 00:15:15.950
So if they get a call, they
can unplug it and answer it,

00:15:15.950 --> 00:15:18.590
but they can't call it with
it while it's charging.

00:15:18.590 --> 00:15:22.220
GUEST SPEAKER 2: I
mean, essentially, they

00:15:22.220 --> 00:15:25.235
use a lot of cell
phone as land lines

00:15:25.235 --> 00:15:27.360
just because they don't
have landline connectivity.

00:15:27.360 --> 00:15:28.700
So yeah.

00:15:33.537 --> 00:15:36.120
GUEST SPEAKER 4: Just a little
bit about the community partner

00:15:36.120 --> 00:15:38.340
we worked with, they
are called Asaprosar.

00:15:38.340 --> 00:15:42.150
They're a human development
NGO based in Santa Ana.

00:15:42.150 --> 00:15:46.980
They work in rural areas and try
to improve the quality of life

00:15:46.980 --> 00:15:50.280
through environmental and
nutritional education, also

00:15:50.280 --> 00:15:54.700
micro-credit opportunities and
general community development.

00:15:54.700 --> 00:15:56.970
One of Asaprosar's
current projects

00:15:56.970 --> 00:16:00.780
is introducing rice
cultivation in El Salvador,

00:16:00.780 --> 00:16:04.680
both as a source of nutrition,
but also, as income generation.

00:16:04.680 --> 00:16:07.020
And in order to prepare
rice for consumption,

00:16:07.020 --> 00:16:10.290
it needs to be threshed
and also dehusked.

00:16:10.290 --> 00:16:12.720
And current methods
that the community uses

00:16:12.720 --> 00:16:14.650
are very time and
money intensive.

00:16:14.650 --> 00:16:17.640
So our design
challenge is to develop

00:16:17.640 --> 00:16:21.660
a cheap and local process that
dehusks rice more successfully

00:16:21.660 --> 00:16:24.300
and conveniently.

00:16:24.300 --> 00:16:27.990
The most important product
attributes that we focused on

00:16:27.990 --> 00:16:31.980
are the efficiency and the
affordability of the product.

00:16:31.980 --> 00:16:36.300
Our goal was to make sure
that more than 90% of the rice

00:16:36.300 --> 00:16:38.880
was successfully
dehusked, while keeping

00:16:38.880 --> 00:16:41.580
the price of the
product under $2.

00:16:41.580 --> 00:16:44.700
We also looked at cost
and durability and safety

00:16:44.700 --> 00:16:45.820
of the product.

00:16:45.820 --> 00:16:49.590
And so after extensive
research and some brainstorming

00:16:49.590 --> 00:16:52.890
exercises, we concluded that
the best approach to this design

00:16:52.890 --> 00:16:55.530
challenge is to modify
an existing molino, which

00:16:55.530 --> 00:16:59.160
is a corn mill currently
used in the communities.

00:16:59.160 --> 00:17:01.620
The advantages are that
it's already available,

00:17:01.620 --> 00:17:04.380
and it's being supplied
by our community partner.

00:17:04.380 --> 00:17:06.240
And essentially,
what we're proposing

00:17:06.240 --> 00:17:09.240
is inserting another material
between the two metal

00:17:09.240 --> 00:17:12.869
plates of the molino that
would provide enough friction

00:17:12.869 --> 00:17:16.589
to dehusk the rice grain,
but at the same time,

00:17:16.589 --> 00:17:19.349
prevent them from being broken.

00:17:19.349 --> 00:17:21.480
And some of the challenges
that we're dealing with

00:17:21.480 --> 00:17:25.530
are the lack of precision
of the molino and then

00:17:25.530 --> 00:17:27.660
also the fact that
it has friction

00:17:27.660 --> 00:17:30.632
so that it wears
the materials down.

00:17:30.632 --> 00:17:33.290
GUEST SPEAKER 5: So I'm going to
talk about the experimentation

00:17:33.290 --> 00:17:33.900
process.

00:17:33.900 --> 00:17:36.350
So initially, our
first problem was

00:17:36.350 --> 00:17:38.850
to decide on what kind of
insert we wanted to use.

00:17:38.850 --> 00:17:41.360
So we looked at metal, various
types of rubber and foam

00:17:41.360 --> 00:17:42.570
and leather.

00:17:42.570 --> 00:17:44.600
And when we were looking
at these materials,

00:17:44.600 --> 00:17:47.200
we really evaluated
them on the cost,

00:17:47.200 --> 00:17:50.030
how much residue do they were
going to leave on the rice,

00:17:50.030 --> 00:17:52.070
how long it was going
to last, or how durable

00:17:52.070 --> 00:17:54.500
and how available
the material was.

00:17:54.500 --> 00:17:57.590
So when we did our experiments,
our two main variables

00:17:57.590 --> 00:17:59.660
that we were looking
at were, essentially,

00:17:59.660 --> 00:18:02.540
the type of material and also
the tightness of the molino.

00:18:02.540 --> 00:18:04.490
So essentially, the
tightness was how much

00:18:04.490 --> 00:18:06.800
space was going to exist
in between the insert

00:18:06.800 --> 00:18:10.110
and the metal plates to
provide the adequate function.

00:18:10.110 --> 00:18:13.550
And when we did our methodology,
our experiments, first, we

00:18:13.550 --> 00:18:16.340
inserted the material, and
we controlled the spacing

00:18:16.340 --> 00:18:20.900
by screwing the tab
all the way in so we

00:18:20.900 --> 00:18:22.490
couldn't turn it any tighter.

00:18:22.490 --> 00:18:25.550
And then we turned it a certain
number of turns back out,

00:18:25.550 --> 00:18:27.920
so we knew how
much space existed

00:18:27.920 --> 00:18:29.697
in between the
[INAUDIBLE] and the space.

00:18:29.697 --> 00:18:31.530
And then we ran the
rice through the molino,

00:18:31.530 --> 00:18:35.160
and after we ran the rice
through, we winnowed it,

00:18:35.160 --> 00:18:37.875
and we separated
the rice into husk.

00:18:37.875 --> 00:18:40.020
But when it was
still husk and if it

00:18:40.020 --> 00:18:43.200
had been dehusked or broken
or the rice particles

00:18:43.200 --> 00:18:46.140
or the leftover husk,
so the three particles

00:18:46.140 --> 00:18:46.920
we were left with.

00:18:46.920 --> 00:18:49.680
And these pictures
show the various types

00:18:49.680 --> 00:18:53.160
of experiments we really did and
was quite an extensive process.

00:18:53.160 --> 00:18:56.880
We experimented with various
different types of materials.

00:18:56.880 --> 00:18:59.760
We would use one material and
varied the number of turns.

00:18:59.760 --> 00:19:03.600
We might insert multiple
layers of the material

00:19:03.600 --> 00:19:05.050
to see if that changed anything.

00:19:05.050 --> 00:19:07.950
And also, we would run
the material, the rice

00:19:07.950 --> 00:19:11.190
through twice and see if
that changed the efficiency

00:19:11.190 --> 00:19:12.490
of the rice itself.

00:19:12.490 --> 00:19:14.370
And ultimately,
what we decided was

00:19:14.370 --> 00:19:17.700
that leather was the most
appropriate insert for us.

00:19:17.700 --> 00:19:20.487
Because when we did
different material inserts,

00:19:20.487 --> 00:19:23.070
we, essentially, found out that
it wasn't so much the material

00:19:23.070 --> 00:19:24.528
that matters, but
it was the number

00:19:24.528 --> 00:19:25.860
of turns that was really--

00:19:25.860 --> 00:19:29.850
when changed the process
and the efficiency.

00:19:29.850 --> 00:19:31.560
And we knew that
this material had

00:19:31.560 --> 00:19:35.370
to be soft enough so that
the frictional and the torque

00:19:35.370 --> 00:19:37.280
wouldn't break the material.

00:19:37.280 --> 00:19:39.780
But we also knew it had to be
very durable because we didn't

00:19:39.780 --> 00:19:43.037
want it to wear away, so leather
was the best option for us.

00:19:43.037 --> 00:19:45.120
GUEST SPEAKER 6: So when
we're looking at leather,

00:19:45.120 --> 00:19:48.810
we first decided that we
wanted to do unattached,

00:19:48.810 --> 00:19:51.810
without having to glue around
here the leather to the molino,

00:19:51.810 --> 00:19:54.270
it allowed for flexibility
for molino use,

00:19:54.270 --> 00:19:56.880
and it had pretty
similar results,

00:19:56.880 --> 00:20:00.480
and it wasn't hazardous if
we're not using some toxic glue.

00:20:00.480 --> 00:20:03.450
We also decided with
brown, organic leather.

00:20:03.450 --> 00:20:06.300
We didn't want anything that
was too processed or patented

00:20:06.300 --> 00:20:07.000
or anything.

00:20:07.000 --> 00:20:09.870
And we thought that one
piece might be acceptable.

00:20:09.870 --> 00:20:12.240
Because it was similar,
we had similar results

00:20:12.240 --> 00:20:13.830
on two or more pieces.

00:20:13.830 --> 00:20:16.140
It's obviously cheaper,
if it's [INAUDIBLE]

00:20:16.140 --> 00:20:19.110
and it's less hazardous if we're
not [INAUDIBLE] around here

00:20:19.110 --> 00:20:21.090
together again.

00:20:21.090 --> 00:20:23.700
So we actually were
worried about toxicity

00:20:23.700 --> 00:20:25.950
after we learned about
tanning those kind of chemical

00:20:25.950 --> 00:20:27.560
intensive.

00:20:27.560 --> 00:20:30.200
We concluded though that
there is no dangerous exposure

00:20:30.200 --> 00:20:32.960
because chromium 6 is the
most hazardous chemical that's

00:20:32.960 --> 00:20:34.220
being used in treatment.

00:20:34.220 --> 00:20:36.950
And it's actually broken
down into chromium 3, which

00:20:36.950 --> 00:20:40.400
is an essential nutrient in
your body, so when it's inhaled,

00:20:40.400 --> 00:20:42.780
it's OK.

00:20:42.780 --> 00:20:45.650
OSHA says that 100 micrograms
of chromium protein or drinking

00:20:45.650 --> 00:20:47.280
water is acceptable.

00:20:47.280 --> 00:20:49.610
We found that that's
equivalent to eating seven

00:20:49.610 --> 00:20:51.500
of these inserts a
day, so we don't think

00:20:51.500 --> 00:20:53.640
anyoe is going to do that.

00:20:53.640 --> 00:20:55.400
We also found that
there's not really

00:20:55.400 --> 00:20:57.170
significant leather ware.

00:20:57.170 --> 00:20:59.310
We process about 7/2
kilograms of rice,

00:20:59.310 --> 00:21:02.720
which is a family's roughly rice
consumption using this concert,

00:21:02.720 --> 00:21:05.360
and I'll pass this
around, but this

00:21:05.360 --> 00:21:08.260
is a new one compared to
that one that we processed.

00:21:08.260 --> 00:21:10.350
So you can tell that it
stretched a little bit,

00:21:10.350 --> 00:21:15.030
but other than that, there's
no real wear that we found.

00:21:15.030 --> 00:21:17.550
So we wanted to optimize the
process after we concluded

00:21:17.550 --> 00:21:20.170
that leather was our material.

00:21:20.170 --> 00:21:22.800
And so we processed about
1.5 liters at a time,

00:21:22.800 --> 00:21:25.920
and we did a whole bunch
of different experiments.

00:21:25.920 --> 00:21:28.800
We really focused on the
optimal tightness in the molino,

00:21:28.800 --> 00:21:30.802
and we did this by
looking at the percent

00:21:30.802 --> 00:21:32.550
that was husked and
the broken grains

00:21:32.550 --> 00:21:37.330
and how fast we
could do 1.5 liters.

00:21:37.330 --> 00:21:41.560
And then we also attached five
washers to the side knobs.

00:21:41.560 --> 00:21:43.650
This is allowed for
stability, it allowed us

00:21:43.650 --> 00:21:46.260
to better control, the
spacing between the metal

00:21:46.260 --> 00:21:50.730
and the leather, so we could
really optimize our tightness.

00:21:50.730 --> 00:21:53.400
So this is the data from
our experimentation,

00:21:53.400 --> 00:21:55.590
so we found that using
the one piece of leather,

00:21:55.590 --> 00:21:59.200
we get 94% to 96% husked,
which is really good.

00:21:59.200 --> 00:22:02.940
And so our optimal outcome was
1% of unattached, four to five

00:22:02.940 --> 00:22:05.440
turns out.

00:22:05.440 --> 00:22:09.040
Looking at our original
project specifications,

00:22:09.040 --> 00:22:11.290
we found that this was
very comparable to what

00:22:11.290 --> 00:22:14.890
we have wanted, and
the durability we

00:22:14.890 --> 00:22:17.280
are positive about a
lot of about 160 pounds.

00:22:17.280 --> 00:22:21.120
So we estimated that
was our minimum.

00:22:21.120 --> 00:22:23.745
So our final conclusion is that
one piece of leather unattached

00:22:23.745 --> 00:22:26.880
to molino is at
best, 4.5 to 5 turns,

00:22:26.880 --> 00:22:29.100
but the user can
vary that depending

00:22:29.100 --> 00:22:33.690
on what they want as broken
versus dehusked grains.

00:22:33.690 --> 00:22:35.460
It's very minimal cost.

00:22:35.460 --> 00:22:38.790
It's about $0.50 per insert.

00:22:38.790 --> 00:22:39.480
It's durable.

00:22:39.480 --> 00:22:42.510
Again, we expected more
molino, but not too much,

00:22:42.510 --> 00:22:44.143
and it's not toxic.

00:22:44.143 --> 00:22:45.560
GUST SPEAKER 7:
So now that you've

00:22:45.560 --> 00:22:48.017
heard a little bit of the
decisions behind the process,

00:22:48.017 --> 00:22:50.600
I just want to take a little bit
through of what it might look

00:22:50.600 --> 00:22:52.320
like for someone
that's in the community

00:22:52.320 --> 00:22:55.378
and what they would have to do
to be able to use this process.

00:22:55.378 --> 00:22:57.920
So first, they would have to
get a hold of a piece of leather

00:22:57.920 --> 00:23:02.720
and cut it to the donut shape
that would fit into the shaft.

00:23:02.720 --> 00:23:04.880
And if the molino body
wasn't set up already,

00:23:04.880 --> 00:23:07.730
they would put it on
the stand and proceed

00:23:07.730 --> 00:23:11.690
to put the leather on
the shaft and put that

00:23:11.690 --> 00:23:14.210
into three of the
main body and then

00:23:14.210 --> 00:23:17.510
continue by adding the
reservoir up on top as well

00:23:17.510 --> 00:23:19.790
as the handle on the side.

00:23:19.790 --> 00:23:24.230
Then the next thing would
be to add these washers so

00:23:24.230 --> 00:23:27.710
that this brace can hold
the shaft in the main body,

00:23:27.710 --> 00:23:29.210
but it can still
be tightened enough

00:23:29.210 --> 00:23:34.110
and still leave enough space
for the leather to fit inside.

00:23:34.110 --> 00:23:37.160
Then they would turn
the knob all the way in

00:23:37.160 --> 00:23:40.670
and then turn out the
specified 4.5 to 5 turns,

00:23:40.670 --> 00:23:42.830
or according to
their preference,

00:23:42.830 --> 00:23:47.750
whether they wanted more broken
rice or more unhusked rice

00:23:47.750 --> 00:23:52.770
and would then crank away and
put the rice in and process.

00:23:52.770 --> 00:23:55.550
And from there,
separate the husks

00:23:55.550 --> 00:23:59.000
from the grains by winnowing it,
using their preferred method.

00:23:59.000 --> 00:24:02.730
And from there,
just as a side note,

00:24:02.730 --> 00:24:06.860
we also found that when
we're doing our more detailed

00:24:06.860 --> 00:24:08.660
experiments, we found that--

00:24:08.660 --> 00:24:11.580
or to separate the broken
rice versus the whole grains,

00:24:11.580 --> 00:24:14.480
we found that when we were
shaking it back and forth

00:24:14.480 --> 00:24:19.587
in the sieves, the unhusked
rice actually came up to the top

00:24:19.587 --> 00:24:21.170
just because it's a
little bit larger.

00:24:21.170 --> 00:24:24.660
And so that gave a natural
separation between the ones

00:24:24.660 --> 00:24:26.910
that still have the husks
on and the ones that didn't.

00:24:26.910 --> 00:24:30.140
And so what we're
going to be suggesting

00:24:30.140 --> 00:24:34.562
is to put the winnowed
rice into a container

00:24:34.562 --> 00:24:36.270
and shake it back and
forth a little bit.

00:24:36.270 --> 00:24:38.600
And from there, the rice
will then collect to the top

00:24:38.600 --> 00:24:41.060
and gets to be able
to scoop it out,

00:24:41.060 --> 00:24:45.020
and that's what gives us our
96% efficiency, and that's

00:24:45.020 --> 00:24:47.520
the other contribution
to the process.

00:24:47.520 --> 00:24:50.360
So from there, the
rice is ready to go,

00:24:50.360 --> 00:24:55.280
and they would just have to
be washed before it's eaten.

00:24:55.280 --> 00:24:58.130
So we just like to thank
[INAUDIBLE] and D-Lab staff

00:24:58.130 --> 00:25:02.030
for helping us out and for
Will Floyd from Windmill Rice

00:25:02.030 --> 00:25:04.103
or giving us our rice.

00:25:04.103 --> 00:25:05.520
So if you guys
have any questions,

00:25:05.520 --> 00:25:07.503
just thank you for listening.

00:25:12.926 --> 00:25:15.578
AUDIENCE: And I would
say you did a good job.

00:25:15.578 --> 00:25:17.370
GUEST SPEAKER 6: How
available is leather--

00:25:17.370 --> 00:25:19.400
GUST SPEAKER 7: So we visited--

00:25:19.400 --> 00:25:20.930
or I guess I didn't.

00:25:20.930 --> 00:25:23.762
These two went to the--

00:25:23.762 --> 00:25:25.220
GUEST SPEAKER 4:
We went to a store

00:25:25.220 --> 00:25:27.840
in one of the town's
nearby these communities,

00:25:27.840 --> 00:25:30.380
and it was a shoe store
that just sold leather

00:25:30.380 --> 00:25:34.500
in massive quantities,
so it is available.

00:25:34.500 --> 00:25:37.033
And the community partner
that we worked with, actually,

00:25:37.033 --> 00:25:38.700
they were the ones
who brought us there,

00:25:38.700 --> 00:25:40.890
so they would be able to
supply the communities

00:25:40.890 --> 00:25:43.690
with the leather.

00:25:43.690 --> 00:25:46.922
AUDIENCE: What fraction of your
rice remains broken at the end?

00:25:46.922 --> 00:25:49.110
GUEST SPEAKER 5:
Probably about a third.

00:25:49.110 --> 00:25:53.400
I mean, what we were
really concerned with was

00:25:53.400 --> 00:25:55.860
how much-- like the
percentage of husk,

00:25:55.860 --> 00:26:00.252
like the husk dries because
you can eat broken rice.

00:26:00.252 --> 00:26:00.960
It's not as nice.

00:26:00.960 --> 00:26:05.130
You would rather not eat
it, but for the most part,

00:26:05.130 --> 00:26:07.560
we would rather err on
the side of dehusking

00:26:07.560 --> 00:26:10.296
most of the rice versus
leaving at husked at all.

00:26:10.296 --> 00:26:13.560
So about a third of it
was probably broken.

00:26:13.560 --> 00:26:20.310
We lost maybe about
between 5% and 10%

00:26:20.310 --> 00:26:23.800
of it was just like
sand, grainy sized.

00:26:23.800 --> 00:26:25.583
So it was pretty good.

00:26:25.583 --> 00:26:27.000
GUEST SPEAKER 6:
We have something

00:26:27.000 --> 00:26:29.040
that people can
adjust the turns to,

00:26:29.040 --> 00:26:31.560
so if they would prefer
having more husked rice

00:26:31.560 --> 00:26:33.510
and just pull it out
themselves, then it

00:26:33.510 --> 00:26:35.160
would probably be less
broken rice because it's not

00:26:35.160 --> 00:26:35.850
going to be as tight.

00:26:35.850 --> 00:26:37.580
It's not going to be
crushing it as much.

00:26:37.580 --> 00:26:38.730
GUEST SPEAKER 4: And
something also, we

00:26:38.730 --> 00:26:40.380
learned from the
community partners

00:26:40.380 --> 00:26:43.140
that when they currently
bring it to a mill

00:26:43.140 --> 00:26:46.650
to have it dehusked, and
when they get it back,

00:26:46.650 --> 00:26:50.940
some pieces do still husks on
and some pieces are broken.

00:26:50.940 --> 00:26:55.790
So what they currently have
is something similar to what

00:26:55.790 --> 00:26:58.190
[INAUDIBLE].

00:26:58.190 --> 00:27:00.380
AUDIENCE: And they could
theoretically cycle

00:27:00.380 --> 00:27:02.233
the non-husked,
stone rice in and--

00:27:02.233 --> 00:27:04.400
GUEST SPEAKER 5: Yeah, just
process it back through.

00:27:04.400 --> 00:27:06.317
That's what we found was
really nice about it.

00:27:06.317 --> 00:27:08.858
We weren't quite expecting this,
but when we were shaking it,

00:27:08.858 --> 00:27:09.740
well, look at that.

00:27:09.740 --> 00:27:12.380
All the husked rice is
right in the center.

00:27:12.380 --> 00:27:13.880
So you just scooped
it all out, just

00:27:13.880 --> 00:27:16.595
put it back with the rice that
hasn't been processed yet.

00:27:16.595 --> 00:27:20.180
AUDIENCE: This sieve that
you used, are those--

00:27:20.180 --> 00:27:23.030
GUST SPEAKER 7: Yeah, so
the fact that this is sieved

00:27:23.030 --> 00:27:24.590
is irrelevant.

00:27:24.590 --> 00:27:26.878
We just happened to use a sieve.

00:27:26.878 --> 00:27:28.295
AUDIENCE: You were
using the sieve

00:27:28.295 --> 00:27:29.110
to take out the broken rice.

00:27:29.110 --> 00:27:29.670
GUST SPEAKER 7: Exactly.

00:27:29.670 --> 00:27:30.170
Yeah.

00:27:30.170 --> 00:27:33.683
And so it would just be a
fairly shallow container.

00:27:33.683 --> 00:27:35.600
So the fact that it's a
sieve doesn't really--

00:27:35.600 --> 00:27:37.648
it isn't the one that's
causing it to separate.

00:27:37.648 --> 00:27:39.440
GUEST SPEAKER 5: But
we did realize though,

00:27:39.440 --> 00:27:41.480
it was really important
that the bottom was

00:27:41.480 --> 00:27:44.540
relatively flat and sturdy and
that the sides wouldn't bend.

00:27:44.540 --> 00:27:46.520
So you wouldn't want
a plastic container

00:27:46.520 --> 00:27:50.180
because you can't get the right
motion for that shaking back

00:27:50.180 --> 00:27:52.100
and forth for it to separate.

00:27:52.100 --> 00:27:53.580
So it felt kind of like--

00:27:53.580 --> 00:27:55.163
GUST SPEAKER 7: But
we just, actually,

00:27:55.163 --> 00:27:58.100
used the same container that we
were winnowing in, so I mean,

00:27:58.100 --> 00:28:02.550
we don't have the huge
platters that they use,

00:28:02.550 --> 00:28:04.160
but that would
definitely be something

00:28:04.160 --> 00:28:05.690
that we'll suggest
to [INAUDIBLE]

00:28:05.690 --> 00:28:07.535
and have them try it
out to see if it works.

00:28:11.810 --> 00:28:13.560
JESSICA ARTILES: Good
afternoon, everyone.

00:28:13.560 --> 00:28:14.660
Thank you for coming.

00:28:14.660 --> 00:28:20.270
Our group project is Barrel
on Fire, Charcoal Desire.

00:28:20.270 --> 00:28:24.710
My name is Jessica Artiles and
along with Vivian Dean and Kurt

00:28:24.710 --> 00:28:28.430
Swoll, we went to Sabana
Grande in Nicaragua,

00:28:28.430 --> 00:28:31.670
and we reintroduced and
re-helped the women make

00:28:31.670 --> 00:28:34.830
charcoal out of agri waste.

00:28:34.830 --> 00:28:38.030
So for context of charcoal
in the communities in Sabana

00:28:38.030 --> 00:28:41.450
Grande, in 2009, a
professor, Amy Banzaert,

00:28:41.450 --> 00:28:43.580
visited the community
and introduces charcoal.

00:28:43.580 --> 00:28:45.810
Over the years, the
knowledge was lost.

00:28:45.810 --> 00:28:48.340
The women lost practice.

00:28:48.340 --> 00:28:49.250
They had challenges.

00:28:49.250 --> 00:28:50.618
They didn't yield briquettes.

00:28:50.618 --> 00:28:52.160
When they tried to
make it, they were

00:28:52.160 --> 00:28:54.950
getting less than 10
briquettes per yield.

00:28:54.950 --> 00:28:57.380
So we went this spring
break to Nicaragua.

00:28:57.380 --> 00:29:00.380
We reintroduced the process
to four women, whom we now

00:29:00.380 --> 00:29:01.880
call the Charcoal Captains.

00:29:01.880 --> 00:29:05.810
And when we were there,
our yield of burns

00:29:05.810 --> 00:29:08.630
were coming up to around
80 because of her burn.

00:29:08.630 --> 00:29:11.360
Since then, we came
back, we redesigned

00:29:11.360 --> 00:29:13.310
the charcoal-making process.

00:29:13.310 --> 00:29:15.230
We made the
innovations, send them

00:29:15.230 --> 00:29:17.000
back our feedback and results.

00:29:17.000 --> 00:29:19.220
And when they've done
it with our new design,

00:29:19.220 --> 00:29:22.070
they're getting up to 110
briquettes per burning.

00:29:22.070 --> 00:29:25.280
So the project is looking
commercially viable for them.

00:29:25.280 --> 00:29:27.020
They're looking at
making the charcoal

00:29:27.020 --> 00:29:30.452
for the restaurant that's on
site to fuel and to cook with.

00:29:30.452 --> 00:29:31.910
They're going to
be making charcoal

00:29:31.910 --> 00:29:34.067
to sell to the community,
and they're actually

00:29:34.067 --> 00:29:35.900
going to be displaying
it in their community

00:29:35.900 --> 00:29:39.290
service in May on how to make
charcoal and how to sell it.

00:29:41.978 --> 00:29:44.670
KURT SWOLL: So the
way that we identified

00:29:44.670 --> 00:29:48.960
the problem was looking at
the current charcoal-burning

00:29:48.960 --> 00:29:51.870
process, it requires
lighting this oil drum

00:29:51.870 --> 00:29:54.900
on fire, which is
packed with dry biomass

00:29:54.900 --> 00:29:57.760
and then sealing both
the bottom and the top.

00:29:57.760 --> 00:30:01.740
So this process, right now,
with the barrel standing

00:30:01.740 --> 00:30:04.450
on top of the three bricks,
is optimized for cost.

00:30:04.450 --> 00:30:06.880
So this is the most
low cost way to do it.

00:30:06.880 --> 00:30:10.500
However, we felt like we could
add improvements that would

00:30:10.500 --> 00:30:12.660
be justified in their cost.

00:30:12.660 --> 00:30:15.370
Because this has
two main problems.

00:30:15.370 --> 00:30:17.140
One is the issue
of heat exposure,

00:30:17.140 --> 00:30:19.890
so the user actually has to
get quite close to the barrel

00:30:19.890 --> 00:30:22.320
to be able to kick out the
bricks from the bottom,

00:30:22.320 --> 00:30:27.990
and the second is the
safety issue, where

00:30:27.990 --> 00:30:32.230
in sealing the bottom of the
barrel, you have to tip it,

00:30:32.230 --> 00:30:37.210
and this could result
in some accidents.

00:30:37.210 --> 00:30:39.980
JESSICA ARTILES: [INAUDIBLE].

00:30:39.980 --> 00:30:41.480
KURT SWOLL: So as
you can see, there

00:30:41.480 --> 00:30:43.580
are flames coming out of
the bottom of the barrel,

00:30:43.580 --> 00:30:46.160
as they're doing this, so
that's the heat problem.

00:30:46.160 --> 00:30:50.060
And then as they're
tipping it, the barrel

00:30:50.060 --> 00:30:54.780
fell over and almost
landed on one of the women.

00:30:54.780 --> 00:30:57.590
So this is a safety
concern, and there was also

00:30:57.590 --> 00:31:00.620
a lot of biomass to
the left of the barrel

00:31:00.620 --> 00:31:02.450
so it could have lit
all that on fire too.

00:31:05.928 --> 00:31:07.470
VIVIAN DEAN: So how
do we solve this?

00:31:07.470 --> 00:31:10.430
Our solution consists
of a circular dugout,

00:31:10.430 --> 00:31:13.890
a slightly smaller in
circumference than the barrel.

00:31:13.890 --> 00:31:17.040
And within that dugout
stands three air channels.

00:31:17.040 --> 00:31:21.990
And within each air channel are
two bricks that are embedded,

00:31:21.990 --> 00:31:24.120
and the barrel simply
sits on top of the ground

00:31:24.120 --> 00:31:29.077
and on top of the bricks
for a very stable setup.

00:31:29.077 --> 00:31:30.660
When we tested our
solution, we wanted

00:31:30.660 --> 00:31:32.970
to make sure that the biomass
can still be lit pretty

00:31:32.970 --> 00:31:34.770
easily through the bottom.

00:31:34.770 --> 00:31:37.230
What we found was that
biomass, actually,

00:31:37.230 --> 00:31:40.980
it's a lot easier because
the air channels actually

00:31:40.980 --> 00:31:45.310
block any wind that could
interfere with the process.

00:31:45.310 --> 00:31:48.540
We also calculated the size
of the air channel needed

00:31:48.540 --> 00:31:50.910
to be to allow the
necessary airflow for a very

00:31:50.910 --> 00:31:53.487
thorough burn.

00:31:53.487 --> 00:31:56.070
You can see on the sides of the
barrel is sealed to the ground

00:31:56.070 --> 00:32:01.320
except for by the air channels,
and because the barrel sits

00:32:01.320 --> 00:32:03.240
on the ground level,
we eliminate the risk

00:32:03.240 --> 00:32:07.230
of having to tip over the barrel
or having to handle or move

00:32:07.230 --> 00:32:09.420
the barrel while it's on fire.

00:32:09.420 --> 00:32:10.980
Eventually, you'll
need to cut off

00:32:10.980 --> 00:32:14.760
the air supply to the biomass
to allow it to carbonize,

00:32:14.760 --> 00:32:17.590
and so the way we figured we
would seal these air channels

00:32:17.590 --> 00:32:18.840
is through these metal plates.

00:32:21.380 --> 00:32:23.900
So the idea behind these
places for the user

00:32:23.900 --> 00:32:27.380
to be able to slide the
metal plate from a distance,

00:32:27.380 --> 00:32:29.660
keeping a safe distance,
so that they're not

00:32:29.660 --> 00:32:31.190
exposed to the heat as much.

00:32:31.190 --> 00:32:34.340
And through several
design iterations,

00:32:34.340 --> 00:32:36.920
this is our final
design we came up with.

00:32:36.920 --> 00:32:39.780
You can check it out.

00:32:39.780 --> 00:32:42.500
It's made out of one piece
of sheet metal, which

00:32:42.500 --> 00:32:45.500
is very readily
available in Nicaragua,

00:32:45.500 --> 00:32:48.973
and the handle is made by
making slits in the sheet metal

00:32:48.973 --> 00:32:50.390
and curving it
around and riveting

00:32:50.390 --> 00:32:53.780
it to connect at the
top, and riveting is very

00:32:53.780 --> 00:32:55.250
available in Nicaragua as well.

00:32:58.090 --> 00:33:00.913
So yeah, we just use metal
shears to cut the sheet metal

00:33:00.913 --> 00:33:01.830
so it's pretty simple.

00:33:04.450 --> 00:33:06.060
We have a video of
Kurt demonstrating

00:33:06.060 --> 00:33:07.426
use of these new metal seals.

00:33:07.426 --> 00:33:09.410
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:33:09.410 --> 00:33:12.150
- All right, add the link.

00:33:12.150 --> 00:33:12.650
Great.

00:33:16.546 --> 00:33:18.840
VIVIAN DEAN: You can see
he does this pretty easily,

00:33:18.840 --> 00:33:21.630
maintained a safe distance,
and it's pretty quick.

00:33:21.630 --> 00:33:22.458
- Go for it.

00:33:22.458 --> 00:33:23.052
Go for it.

00:33:26.016 --> 00:33:29.699
- [INAUDIBLE].

00:33:29.699 --> 00:33:30.282
[END PLAYBACK]

00:33:30.282 --> 00:33:32.877
VIVIAN DEAN: And
that is our solution.

00:33:32.877 --> 00:33:34.960
A big question that came
up when sealing the grill

00:33:34.960 --> 00:33:36.400
was whether or
not we should seal

00:33:36.400 --> 00:33:38.020
the bottom first
or the top first.

00:33:38.020 --> 00:33:39.520
And we found through
experimentation

00:33:39.520 --> 00:33:42.640
that it was easiest to first
seal using metal seals.

00:33:42.640 --> 00:33:45.592
So we cut off air flow so
the flames aren't as intense

00:33:45.592 --> 00:33:47.050
coming out of the
top, and then you

00:33:47.050 --> 00:33:49.930
can throw on the lid
on the top, and then

00:33:49.930 --> 00:33:53.080
that allows for smoke to come
out of any air gaps or air

00:33:53.080 --> 00:33:56.730
holes, so you know where
exactly to plug sand in.

00:33:56.730 --> 00:33:57.970
And now, to the solution.

00:33:57.970 --> 00:34:00.280
We actually delivered
this to the [INAUDIBLE],,

00:34:00.280 --> 00:34:03.265
and Jessica will tell you
about any feedback we got.

00:34:03.265 --> 00:34:05.410
JESSICA ARTILES: So our
group is unique in that we

00:34:05.410 --> 00:34:08.272
are able to have a native
speaker, me, to communicate

00:34:08.272 --> 00:34:09.355
with the women constantly.

00:34:09.355 --> 00:34:11.625
We kept communication
with them, through emails,

00:34:11.625 --> 00:34:13.000
phone calls to
their cell phones,

00:34:13.000 --> 00:34:16.310
to various women
and men on site.

00:34:16.310 --> 00:34:19.610
And so this is a picture of
when they did their own barrel

00:34:19.610 --> 00:34:20.110
dugout.

00:34:20.110 --> 00:34:22.969
Again, we had our design,
and we submitted it to them

00:34:22.969 --> 00:34:27.400
on a document of here's what you
should do, new and innovative.

00:34:27.400 --> 00:34:30.170
And so they did the barrel
dugout, and they tried it.

00:34:30.170 --> 00:34:33.340
They made their own sheet metal
designs to cover the barrel,

00:34:33.340 --> 00:34:36.005
so we only send pictures of
ours, but they went ahead

00:34:36.005 --> 00:34:38.380
and they said, we're already
going to make another design

00:34:38.380 --> 00:34:39.159
iteration.

00:34:39.159 --> 00:34:40.360
We love the idea.

00:34:40.360 --> 00:34:42.489
We asked them questions
about how do you

00:34:42.489 --> 00:34:43.780
feel about the safety risk?

00:34:43.780 --> 00:34:46.113
And they said there's no risk
of the barrel falling over

00:34:46.113 --> 00:34:49.210
now, which is just as
important as the heat.

00:34:49.210 --> 00:34:52.900
We asked them how do you like
cooking with the charcoal now.

00:34:52.900 --> 00:34:54.550
They said, I cook
tortillas, which

00:34:54.550 --> 00:34:56.258
is one of the most
valued foods, and they

00:34:56.258 --> 00:34:59.110
said, with the charcoal we make,
the tortillas were delicious.

00:34:59.110 --> 00:35:01.030
I made a connection
with the charcoal.

00:35:01.030 --> 00:35:02.800
They're going to be using--

00:35:02.800 --> 00:35:05.080
arroz con leche, which
is like a rice pudding,

00:35:05.080 --> 00:35:07.070
they're going to be
using the charcoal.

00:35:07.070 --> 00:35:09.700
Now, that they feel safe
enough to continue to use it,

00:35:09.700 --> 00:35:11.920
they're going to be
using it in the community

00:35:11.920 --> 00:35:13.780
here this mid-May.

00:35:13.780 --> 00:35:16.600
And lighting the biomass
is also an added advantage

00:35:16.600 --> 00:35:17.920
because it was very fast.

00:35:17.920 --> 00:35:20.050
All they have to do
is align the holes

00:35:20.050 --> 00:35:22.480
on the bottom of the barrel
with the air channels.

00:35:22.480 --> 00:35:25.120
And now, when they went to
light it, it was easier to light

00:35:25.120 --> 00:35:28.190
because it was with protection.

00:35:28.190 --> 00:35:30.340
So in conclusion,
in summary, we've

00:35:30.340 --> 00:35:32.290
taken the
charcoal-making process

00:35:32.290 --> 00:35:35.800
and added four points
of making it easier.

00:35:35.800 --> 00:35:37.480
Four innovative
points to the process.

00:35:37.480 --> 00:35:39.790
We've made the lighting
of the barrel easier

00:35:39.790 --> 00:35:42.400
because now, when you're getting
the match under the barrel,

00:35:42.400 --> 00:35:45.340
it's wind protected, so
the wind won't put it out.

00:35:45.340 --> 00:35:48.250
Now, while the barrel is burning
for those 15, sometimes 25

00:35:48.250 --> 00:35:50.200
minutes, it's cooler.

00:35:50.200 --> 00:35:52.780
The flames aren't coming out
from under the bottom radially

00:35:52.780 --> 00:35:54.160
out towards your feet.

00:35:54.160 --> 00:35:57.020
Now, the ceiling of
the barrel is safer

00:35:57.020 --> 00:35:59.020
because there's no longer
a tip and kick method.

00:35:59.020 --> 00:36:01.000
All you got to do is
use the metal sheets,

00:36:01.000 --> 00:36:03.610
and it's faster to seal as well.

00:36:03.610 --> 00:36:05.380
Because it doesn't
require coordination,

00:36:05.380 --> 00:36:07.270
and all you got to do
is slip the metal sheet

00:36:07.270 --> 00:36:08.636
like you saw in our video.

00:36:11.354 --> 00:36:14.400
KURT SWOLL: So throughout
the process of developing

00:36:14.400 --> 00:36:16.550
the solution, we
did some experiments

00:36:16.550 --> 00:36:18.650
around where the
heat was coming from,

00:36:18.650 --> 00:36:20.060
and we discovered
that most of it

00:36:20.060 --> 00:36:23.430
was coming from thermal
radiation from the barrel.

00:36:23.430 --> 00:36:25.730
So we thought, OK,
a way that we could

00:36:25.730 --> 00:36:27.860
improve our solution
even more is

00:36:27.860 --> 00:36:30.380
to develop radiation shields.

00:36:30.380 --> 00:36:34.550
And the gold standard
of radiation shields

00:36:34.550 --> 00:36:36.950
is aluminum tin foil,
but we ruled this out

00:36:36.950 --> 00:36:39.590
because it is just too
brittle and wouldn't

00:36:39.590 --> 00:36:43.100
be able to withstand the thermal
cycles of a barrel burning

00:36:43.100 --> 00:36:44.030
again and again.

00:36:44.030 --> 00:36:47.750
So we experimented with
two others as well.

00:36:47.750 --> 00:36:49.880
Here, you have window
screening, this

00:36:49.880 --> 00:36:52.490
is made out of aluminum,
and galvanized steel,

00:36:52.490 --> 00:36:53.570
like metal mesh.

00:36:53.570 --> 00:36:58.880
And the conclusion was that
it wouldn't justify the cost.

00:36:58.880 --> 00:37:01.010
The heat reduction
wouldn't justify the cost.

00:37:01.010 --> 00:37:04.670
So you see in this graph is
the cost of insulation going up

00:37:04.670 --> 00:37:07.460
on the x-axis, and
that's as measured

00:37:07.460 --> 00:37:10.670
by more and more layers
of either the metal

00:37:10.670 --> 00:37:14.390
sheet or the window screen.

00:37:14.390 --> 00:37:18.140
And on the y-axis, you have
percentage of heat reduction.

00:37:18.140 --> 00:37:20.610
So you do see a
positive correlation.

00:37:20.610 --> 00:37:24.110
The more of that
insulation that you put on,

00:37:24.110 --> 00:37:25.460
the more heat reduction you get.

00:37:25.460 --> 00:37:28.520
However, spending upwards
of $15 doesn't even

00:37:28.520 --> 00:37:30.380
get you 40% reduction.

00:37:34.170 --> 00:37:35.820
So where does this
leave our solution?

00:37:35.820 --> 00:37:40.080
Well, we think that the dugout
and metal plate seals really

00:37:40.080 --> 00:37:43.950
represent kind of a sweet spot,
like in a range of solutions.

00:37:43.950 --> 00:37:48.330
At the very top, you just
have the tip and kick method.

00:37:48.330 --> 00:37:51.450
And then you can
introduce the dugout

00:37:51.450 --> 00:37:53.940
and just seal the
holes with sand,

00:37:53.940 --> 00:37:58.080
but that wouldn't give you
as much heat reduction.

00:37:58.080 --> 00:38:00.615
And ours is medium cost.

00:38:00.615 --> 00:38:02.490
The sheet metal is not
that expensive to make

00:38:02.490 --> 00:38:05.040
the plates out of, and it
gets you a significant heat

00:38:05.040 --> 00:38:05.880
reduction.

00:38:05.880 --> 00:38:09.720
And finally, adding some
kind of radiation shields

00:38:09.720 --> 00:38:14.850
is pretty high cost, and gives
you more reduction in heat,

00:38:14.850 --> 00:38:19.720
but we don't think that it
really justifies the cost.

00:38:19.720 --> 00:38:22.690
So we have a lot
of people to thank.

00:38:22.690 --> 00:38:24.330
I won't read them
all out because I

00:38:24.330 --> 00:38:26.230
think we are just about done.

00:38:26.230 --> 00:38:29.950
But we are looking forward to
your questions and any feedback

00:38:29.950 --> 00:38:30.810
you might have.

00:38:30.810 --> 00:38:32.260
AUDIENCE: Do you
have an estimate

00:38:32.260 --> 00:38:35.830
on how much each
metal sheet would cost

00:38:35.830 --> 00:38:38.542
and how long you
think it would last?

00:38:38.542 --> 00:38:40.250
JESSICA ARTILES: That's
a great question.

00:38:40.250 --> 00:38:43.380
We thought about that a lot,
but when we introduced--

00:38:43.380 --> 00:38:43.970
so we don't.

00:38:43.970 --> 00:38:45.595
But when we introduced
the sheet metal,

00:38:45.595 --> 00:38:47.250
they said, sure, we'll do it.

00:38:47.250 --> 00:38:50.060
And again, they made one set of
this design of the sheet metal,

00:38:50.060 --> 00:38:53.990
and they're ready to
make another set, so

00:38:53.990 --> 00:38:56.240
another batch of
supplies to make

00:38:56.240 --> 00:38:57.980
another set of sheet metals.

00:38:57.980 --> 00:39:02.210
So it doesn't seem like it's
a restrictive cost for them.

00:39:02.210 --> 00:39:06.920
KURT SWOLL: That is one thing
that we neglected to look into,

00:39:06.920 --> 00:39:08.840
but we know it's there
on site because they

00:39:08.840 --> 00:39:12.600
used this type of sheet metal
for their solar [INAUDIBLE]..

00:39:12.600 --> 00:39:14.582
So we know they can obtain it.

00:39:14.582 --> 00:39:16.040
VIVIAN DEAN: And
I don't think they

00:39:16.040 --> 00:39:18.317
had to go out and
buy it for anything

00:39:18.317 --> 00:39:20.150
because they already
had it in their center.

00:39:20.150 --> 00:39:22.880
So our case is kind of
special in that they

00:39:22.880 --> 00:39:25.706
do have these resources.

00:39:25.706 --> 00:39:28.220
AUDIENCE: It seems like
the topping method still

00:39:28.220 --> 00:39:29.720
involves getting
close to the barrel

00:39:29.720 --> 00:39:32.540
where everything else maintains
a distance from the barrel.

00:39:32.540 --> 00:39:36.440
Have you considered just putting
a top riveting, a small handle

00:39:36.440 --> 00:39:38.780
on top of the top piece
so that you could also

00:39:38.780 --> 00:39:39.950
put that on with your stick?

00:39:39.950 --> 00:39:41.742
JESSICA ARTILES: That's
really interesting.

00:39:41.742 --> 00:39:43.850
Actually, in the design
that the women made--

00:39:43.850 --> 00:39:44.900
that's a good
question, by the way.

00:39:44.900 --> 00:39:47.108
And the design that the
women made in Nicaragua, they

00:39:47.108 --> 00:39:48.800
put a handle on
their metal sheet,

00:39:48.800 --> 00:39:51.320
and they said that for our
second generation that they're

00:39:51.320 --> 00:39:53.153
going to make, they're
going to take out the

00:39:53.153 --> 00:39:56.090
handle because they realized
that it's unnecessary.

00:39:56.090 --> 00:39:57.442
They said it's not that hot.

00:39:57.442 --> 00:39:59.900
You can get close to it and
just insert it with your hands.

00:39:59.900 --> 00:40:00.983
That's what they're doing.

00:40:00.983 --> 00:40:03.380
So theirs doesn't even have
the little hole or the left

00:40:03.380 --> 00:40:05.240
handle that we've
made for our designs.

00:40:05.240 --> 00:40:06.680
So they're OK even without it

00:40:06.680 --> 00:40:08.420
KURT SWOLL: Well, I think you
were talking about the top,

00:40:08.420 --> 00:40:08.810
right?

00:40:08.810 --> 00:40:08.960
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

00:40:08.960 --> 00:40:10.502
KURT SWOLL: Yeah,
they have one where

00:40:10.502 --> 00:40:13.130
it's like the circular metal
plate that we have here,

00:40:13.130 --> 00:40:15.740
and there's basically,
like a pot handle over it.

00:40:15.740 --> 00:40:18.410
You just stick the stick
in, then you put it over.

00:40:18.410 --> 00:40:20.650
So they have that.

00:40:20.650 --> 00:40:22.590
AUDIENCE: OK, you
stayed on time,

00:40:22.590 --> 00:40:24.740
so I'm going to
cancel questions.

00:40:24.740 --> 00:40:25.928
Thank you very much, guys,

00:40:25.928 --> 00:40:26.610
JESSICA ARTILES: Well,
you know where to find us,

00:40:26.610 --> 00:40:27.200
if you have any more.

00:40:27.200 --> 00:40:28.575
VIVIAN DEAN: We're
in the corner.

00:40:28.575 --> 00:40:30.300
You can ask those questions.

00:40:30.300 --> 00:40:30.870
Thank you.

00:40:30.870 --> 00:40:31.578
AUDIENCE: Thanks.

00:40:36.890 --> 00:40:38.535
BUS LEONARD: We're team OJ.

00:40:38.535 --> 00:40:40.725
I'd first I'd like to
introduce the team.

00:40:40.725 --> 00:40:41.813
I'm Bus Leonard.

00:40:41.813 --> 00:40:43.105
BRIANNA CONRAD: Brianna Conrad.

00:40:43.105 --> 00:40:43.560
EMILY ROSSER: Hi.

00:40:43.560 --> 00:40:44.460
I'm Emily Rosser.

00:40:44.460 --> 00:40:46.180
CARTER CHANG: And Carter Chang.

00:40:46.180 --> 00:40:52.800
BUS LEONARD: So our project is
sealing the plastic bag sealer

00:40:52.800 --> 00:40:56.310
that we developed during
our trip down to Nicaragua

00:40:56.310 --> 00:40:59.748
over spring break when we were
in fact finding and analyzing

00:40:59.748 --> 00:41:01.540
four different projects
we were looking at.

00:41:01.540 --> 00:41:03.623
And we chose to develop
this one for the remainder

00:41:03.623 --> 00:41:06.257
of the semester, for
a variety of reasons.

00:41:06.257 --> 00:41:08.340
First, I'd like to describe
the situation we found

00:41:08.340 --> 00:41:11.550
in El Roblar, which is the
community that we visited

00:41:11.550 --> 00:41:12.900
in Nicaragua.

00:41:12.900 --> 00:41:18.150
They have a large orange crop
that they get twice a year,

00:41:18.150 --> 00:41:23.190
and it's very imaginable that
every similar community has

00:41:23.190 --> 00:41:25.200
that same orange crop,
so they can't really

00:41:25.200 --> 00:41:28.110
sell those oranges
on any type of market

00:41:28.110 --> 00:41:30.910
because the market is flooded.

00:41:30.910 --> 00:41:34.560
So the goal is to basically,
develop a way for them

00:41:34.560 --> 00:41:38.100
to preserve that orange
product, and for us, it

00:41:38.100 --> 00:41:41.010
came in the form of juice,
so they can sell when

00:41:41.010 --> 00:41:45.660
there is a market for oranges.

00:41:45.660 --> 00:41:48.300
So we presented.

00:41:48.300 --> 00:41:51.570
This project was the first
developed in last year's class,

00:41:51.570 --> 00:41:55.650
and we presented that
version in El Roblar

00:41:55.650 --> 00:41:59.160
to the women of the community
who will be using this product.

00:41:59.160 --> 00:42:01.980
And the presentation,
I thought we

00:42:01.980 --> 00:42:04.560
had some technical difficulties
because we went down

00:42:04.560 --> 00:42:07.800
expecting to be able to run
the device off of a 12-volt car

00:42:07.800 --> 00:42:11.160
battery, but when
we got there, we

00:42:11.160 --> 00:42:13.890
realized that since the last
group had gone down last spring

00:42:13.890 --> 00:42:16.620
break, they had just
installed a micro hydro grid,

00:42:16.620 --> 00:42:20.260
and they now had 120 volts AC
coming out of a wall socket.

00:42:20.260 --> 00:42:23.640
And so nobody really had
the total car batteries

00:42:23.640 --> 00:42:25.180
for us the power of the device.

00:42:25.180 --> 00:42:27.720
So we strung up a bunch of
double A and triple A batteries

00:42:27.720 --> 00:42:30.240
to be able to do a
couple of bag seals.

00:42:30.240 --> 00:42:33.690
And there was a lot of
excitement about this device

00:42:33.690 --> 00:42:34.854
while we were down there.

00:42:34.854 --> 00:42:38.508
You can see we
gathered around it,

00:42:38.508 --> 00:42:40.300
but we can only do a
couple of bags sealers

00:42:40.300 --> 00:42:42.010
for the AA batteries
that we just

00:42:42.010 --> 00:42:46.090
happen to have in our
backpacks were totally drained.

00:42:46.090 --> 00:42:50.140
So that's basically
just the project,

00:42:50.140 --> 00:42:52.030
to be able to create
something that they

00:42:52.030 --> 00:42:54.070
can use in this
community and also,

00:42:54.070 --> 00:42:56.457
be adapted to other communities
so that they can seal

00:42:56.457 --> 00:42:58.040
orange juice or
whatever kind of thing

00:42:58.040 --> 00:42:59.165
they're trying to preserve.

00:43:02.197 --> 00:43:03.780
And they have a
pasteurization process

00:43:03.780 --> 00:43:08.550
that comes before this
bag sealing that they use.

00:43:08.550 --> 00:43:13.060
So this bag sealing is the
second step of that process.

00:43:13.060 --> 00:43:16.570
And we worked with AsoFenix,
which is a Nicaraguan NGO.

00:43:16.570 --> 00:43:19.210
They do sustainable
development and energy

00:43:19.210 --> 00:43:22.450
work with many rural
communities in the area,

00:43:22.450 --> 00:43:25.240
and I'll pass this
off to Rosser.

00:43:25.240 --> 00:43:28.420
EMILY ROSSER: So AsoFenix,
we're incredibly grateful for

00:43:28.420 --> 00:43:29.420
and helped us out a lot.

00:43:29.420 --> 00:43:32.680
However, they rendered the
first prototype obsolete.

00:43:32.680 --> 00:43:34.420
So we had a couple
of improvements

00:43:34.420 --> 00:43:36.320
to make after this.

00:43:36.320 --> 00:43:37.920
This was the first version.

00:43:37.920 --> 00:43:41.290
As you can see, a kind of
permanent circuit that was

00:43:41.290 --> 00:43:43.780
prefabricated, the
most expensive part

00:43:43.780 --> 00:43:44.860
of the entire device--

00:43:44.860 --> 00:43:47.860
a hinge that they had bought
and a latch that was of

00:43:47.860 --> 00:43:49.390
sticky to pull.

00:43:49.390 --> 00:43:54.620
Therefore, we wanted to
remedy this in version 2.0.

00:43:54.620 --> 00:43:57.190
We also wanted to incorporate
a few of the things

00:43:57.190 --> 00:43:58.645
that we learned in Nicaragua.

00:43:58.645 --> 00:44:01.370
A, that there is not really
a reliable power source.

00:44:01.370 --> 00:44:04.870
We wanted to promote
both DC and AC needs.

00:44:04.870 --> 00:44:08.830
To do this, we got
an adapter that

00:44:08.830 --> 00:44:13.480
can go from the wall outlet,
wall AC power, and output 12 DC

00:44:13.480 --> 00:44:14.500
volts.

00:44:14.500 --> 00:44:16.900
Also, you can just
take those same leads

00:44:16.900 --> 00:44:18.820
and put them up to
a 12-DC-volt battery

00:44:18.820 --> 00:44:21.010
and achieve the same effect.

00:44:21.010 --> 00:44:23.920
Second help was this
pasteurization process.

00:44:23.920 --> 00:44:27.850
We didn't really know much about
it for the initial prototype,

00:44:27.850 --> 00:44:30.610
but after going to
El Roblar, we did

00:44:30.610 --> 00:44:33.290
learn that the pasteurization
process is there.

00:44:33.290 --> 00:44:35.560
We just have to
get the oxygen out.

00:44:35.560 --> 00:44:38.140
So we had these two
parameters in place

00:44:38.140 --> 00:44:41.870
while we were trying
to think of the design.

00:44:41.870 --> 00:44:44.650
So the air removal process,
getting all the air out,

00:44:44.650 --> 00:44:47.710
and ergonomics were also
factors in the design,

00:44:47.710 --> 00:44:49.540
and this would lead
to us exploring

00:44:49.540 --> 00:44:51.713
several different orientations.

00:44:51.713 --> 00:44:53.380
First off, a vertical
sealer, whether it

00:44:53.380 --> 00:44:55.930
was hanging off from the
edge of a table or dismounted

00:44:55.930 --> 00:44:57.070
like that.

00:44:57.070 --> 00:44:59.110
It wasn't really conducive
to user friendliness.

00:44:59.110 --> 00:45:00.790
You had to hold
the bag in place.

00:45:00.790 --> 00:45:03.820
It was of difficult
to get a clean seal

00:45:03.820 --> 00:45:05.680
without any air in it.

00:45:05.680 --> 00:45:07.840
It wasn't really the best.

00:45:07.840 --> 00:45:09.910
Next, a handheld device,
kind of like a clamp

00:45:09.910 --> 00:45:13.060
that you can just put in
place on the hanging bag.

00:45:13.060 --> 00:45:14.570
It had a lot of moving parts.

00:45:14.570 --> 00:45:16.870
We felt it wouldn't be the
easiest thing to scrounge

00:45:16.870 --> 00:45:18.190
for those parts in Nicaragua.

00:45:18.190 --> 00:45:22.120
It was kind of a lot of
fabrication involved.

00:45:22.120 --> 00:45:25.690
Last but not least, we had a
horizontal sealer most similar

00:45:25.690 --> 00:45:28.810
to version 1.0, the
prototype last year.

00:45:28.810 --> 00:45:31.240
Our biggest problem with
this was A, it messy.

00:45:31.240 --> 00:45:34.510
It got orange juice that
was running off everywhere,

00:45:34.510 --> 00:45:38.860
and it was harder to get a
airtight seal as a result.

00:45:38.860 --> 00:45:41.230
Also, just ergonomics,
pressing on it again

00:45:41.230 --> 00:45:44.210
and again and again
was kind of difficult.

00:45:44.210 --> 00:45:47.090
So in order to make the
best of both worlds,

00:45:47.090 --> 00:45:49.960
we decided to combine both
vertical sealing and horizontal

00:45:49.960 --> 00:45:52.120
sealing, the two most
feasible, and put it

00:45:52.120 --> 00:45:55.410
at 45 degrees, a perfect
split at the offense.

00:45:55.410 --> 00:45:56.710
Anyway.

00:45:56.710 --> 00:45:59.170
So this is the CAD
model of our design.

00:45:59.170 --> 00:46:01.780
The reason we wanted
to put it at 45 degrees

00:46:01.780 --> 00:46:05.200
was to allow a path for the
runoff to go into a bowl

00:46:05.200 --> 00:46:07.780
underneath, hence,
also why it's elevated.

00:46:07.780 --> 00:46:11.350
Also, it's easier to smooth out
the bag and get all the air out

00:46:11.350 --> 00:46:14.080
when you're doing this, a lot
easier to chop up the water

00:46:14.080 --> 00:46:16.720
level or put the arm
down at the water level

00:46:16.720 --> 00:46:18.490
and get all the air out.

00:46:18.490 --> 00:46:22.600
So with this design in
mind, we eventually led to--

00:46:22.600 --> 00:46:25.340
CARTER CHANG: --our
final product.

00:46:25.340 --> 00:46:27.460
So as you can see
our final product.

00:46:27.460 --> 00:46:30.580
is We decided on the 45-degree
angle for feasibility

00:46:30.580 --> 00:46:33.680
and just for functionality.

00:46:33.680 --> 00:46:36.580
One of our great
improvements is the latch,

00:46:36.580 --> 00:46:39.760
so originally, the latch
was a wireless hanging out

00:46:39.760 --> 00:46:41.510
that people would
have to manually pull

00:46:41.510 --> 00:46:43.040
with a significant
amount of force.

00:46:43.040 --> 00:46:48.585
But in this orientation, we
have a easy spring-held latch,

00:46:48.585 --> 00:46:50.335
where all we have to
do is click and push.

00:46:50.335 --> 00:46:54.800
It opens the OJ sealing.

00:46:54.800 --> 00:46:58.051
And another improvement
is the circuitry.

00:46:58.051 --> 00:47:01.390
BRIANNA CONRAD:
So first of all, I

00:47:01.390 --> 00:47:04.200
want to talk about
the power adapter.

00:47:04.200 --> 00:47:08.020
As you can see, we just bought
a plug-into-the-wall power

00:47:08.020 --> 00:47:08.520
adapter.

00:47:08.520 --> 00:47:11.940
We did look into making
our own or running things

00:47:11.940 --> 00:47:14.700
without bringing the
power down, but we

00:47:14.700 --> 00:47:19.170
found that this was the simplest
option and comparable in price

00:47:19.170 --> 00:47:20.940
to any others, so
we ended up going

00:47:20.940 --> 00:47:23.490
with the buy a premade one.

00:47:23.490 --> 00:47:27.500
The circuit in the previous
design was very expensive.

00:47:27.500 --> 00:47:29.740
It was a premade tiny board.

00:47:29.740 --> 00:47:34.218
And one of the places that
the other team had actually

00:47:34.218 --> 00:47:35.760
thought of as a
place for improvement

00:47:35.760 --> 00:47:39.930
was to design a separate
timing circuit, so we did that.

00:47:39.930 --> 00:47:44.550
We put it on the side here,
so the LED is visible.

00:47:44.550 --> 00:47:51.120
It's just an LM 555 timer, if
anyone is familiar with those.

00:47:51.120 --> 00:47:53.220
And the circuitry to
make it so that we

00:47:53.220 --> 00:47:57.480
can change the time from about
3 seconds to about 30 seconds

00:47:57.480 --> 00:47:59.550
for different
temperatures of juice

00:47:59.550 --> 00:48:03.360
and thicknesses of
plastic and stuff.

00:48:03.360 --> 00:48:08.350
So the wheel for changing
it is actually in there.

00:48:08.350 --> 00:48:10.920
So you would set it, get
it right for one day,

00:48:10.920 --> 00:48:12.870
and then put it back up.

00:48:12.870 --> 00:48:18.480
And you turn it on by hitting a
button that sets off the timer.

00:48:18.480 --> 00:48:21.480
There's a light here
that would go on.

00:48:21.480 --> 00:48:26.530
We've actually got
it so the timer turns

00:48:26.530 --> 00:48:29.270
on the micro heating element
for a certain amount of time,

00:48:29.270 --> 00:48:30.190
which melts the bag.

00:48:30.190 --> 00:48:32.620
And then the LED will stay
on for about 5 seconds

00:48:32.620 --> 00:48:37.480
after the micro
heat has turned off.

00:48:37.480 --> 00:48:40.000
Because waiting
5 seconds is good

00:48:40.000 --> 00:48:43.123
so that the plastic
isn't still partly melted

00:48:43.123 --> 00:48:45.790
when you're trying to pick it up
and so you don't burn yourself.

00:48:45.790 --> 00:48:48.533
And we found that
counting it on your own

00:48:48.533 --> 00:48:50.200
was very difficult,
especially if you're

00:48:50.200 --> 00:48:52.575
excited about sealing your
first bags of juice and stuff.

00:48:52.575 --> 00:48:58.150
So we made the LED stay on
longer to give a very clear

00:48:58.150 --> 00:49:00.180
this is when you take it out.

00:49:00.180 --> 00:49:02.770
CARTER CHANG: Looking at
the cost of our product,

00:49:02.770 --> 00:49:05.530
originally, the version
1.0 cost about $56

00:49:05.530 --> 00:49:06.910
for all the materials.

00:49:06.910 --> 00:49:09.670
We were able to cut that
in half with the additional

00:49:09.670 --> 00:49:11.320
functionality of our products.

00:49:11.320 --> 00:49:13.720
We were able to ensure
that airtight seal,

00:49:13.720 --> 00:49:17.500
in addition to have a
very usable latch system,

00:49:17.500 --> 00:49:19.450
in addition to a
great timing circuit

00:49:19.450 --> 00:49:24.880
that's compatible with
their AC power down there.

00:49:27.470 --> 00:49:30.460
In addition to that, in
terms of implementation,

00:49:30.460 --> 00:49:35.090
we plan to deliver our product
to AsoFenix via Amy in August,

00:49:35.090 --> 00:49:36.050
I believe.

00:49:36.050 --> 00:49:37.670
It will bring the
product down there.

00:49:37.670 --> 00:49:40.480
In addition to that, we
will have a user manual

00:49:40.480 --> 00:49:43.570
in English and Spanish
with user specs in addition

00:49:43.570 --> 00:49:47.440
to additional circuits just
in case needing repairs.

00:49:47.440 --> 00:49:51.400
And then also [INAUDIBLE] help
distribute to the end users.

00:49:51.400 --> 00:49:57.730
We'd like to thank AsoFenix, our
mentor, Ken, Amy, Amit, D-Lab,

00:49:57.730 --> 00:49:59.880
MIT, as well as the
Microsoft [INAUDIBLE]

00:49:59.880 --> 00:50:01.870
for allowing us to
use their venues.

00:50:01.870 --> 00:50:02.980
Are there any questions?

00:50:06.354 --> 00:50:08.400
AUDIENCE: Do you have bags?

00:50:08.400 --> 00:50:09.380
Can we see that now?

00:50:09.380 --> 00:50:11.205
CARTER CHANG: Yeah.

00:50:11.205 --> 00:50:13.260
EMILY ROSSER: If we can get a--

00:50:13.260 --> 00:50:14.745
oh, sweet.

00:50:14.745 --> 00:50:18.000
CARTER CHANG: [INAUDIBLE].

00:50:18.000 --> 00:50:19.120
EMILY ROSSER: Everything.

00:50:19.120 --> 00:50:21.285
Let's move their awesome
[INAUDIBLE] a little

00:50:21.285 --> 00:50:21.873
farther away.

00:50:21.873 --> 00:50:23.640
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:50:23.640 --> 00:50:25.320
BRIANNA CONRAD: This is dark.

00:50:25.320 --> 00:50:27.926
Actually, someone
else can pour it in.

00:50:27.926 --> 00:50:30.180
EMILY ROSSER: This is our
homemade, Nicaraguan orange

00:50:30.180 --> 00:50:32.370
juice flown in
fresh this morning.

00:50:32.370 --> 00:50:38.110
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:50:38.110 --> 00:50:40.440
CARTER CHANG: So we
have the OJ in the bag.

00:50:40.440 --> 00:50:44.235
We want to put a bowl here just
in case there's excess spill.

00:50:44.235 --> 00:50:46.740
BUS LEONARD: So we have
this wire that's, obviously,

00:50:46.740 --> 00:50:48.840
we're not using all of
the heating capacity

00:50:48.840 --> 00:50:50.520
because we don't know
what size of bags

00:50:50.520 --> 00:50:51.630
that they'll actually use.

00:50:51.630 --> 00:50:53.310
They can be doing
personal-size bags

00:50:53.310 --> 00:50:56.530
that they sell on buses or
other similar situations.

00:50:56.530 --> 00:50:59.620
Or they could be making kind of
more wholesale litter-size bags

00:50:59.620 --> 00:51:03.197
that they sell to
restaurants or something.

00:51:03.197 --> 00:51:04.030
So that's going to--

00:51:04.030 --> 00:51:05.980
CARTER CHANG: So have
the long micro wire.

00:51:05.980 --> 00:51:07.060
Here's the bag here.

00:51:07.060 --> 00:51:10.090
We have a little
guiding arm, so this arm

00:51:10.090 --> 00:51:13.700
doesn't jiggle back and forth.

00:51:13.700 --> 00:51:18.540
So we create our seal by
sealing it through the juice.

00:51:18.540 --> 00:51:22.020
It's down here with excess
juice coming out into the bowl,

00:51:22.020 --> 00:51:25.152
and you press the
button, the heat goes on.

00:51:25.152 --> 00:51:28.260
And we should wait for it
to tell us when it's ready.

00:51:32.540 --> 00:51:34.300
EMILY ROSSER: So we've
adjusted the timing

00:51:34.300 --> 00:51:36.940
because we've been playing
with these bags all day.

00:51:36.940 --> 00:51:38.470
The idea being
that you won't have

00:51:38.470 --> 00:51:41.170
to adjust it if you're doing
large batches of bagging

00:51:41.170 --> 00:51:41.837
your juice.

00:51:41.837 --> 00:51:43.420
BRIANNA CONRAD: We
haven't adjusted it

00:51:43.420 --> 00:51:45.310
since we left
[INAUDIBLE] this morning.

00:51:45.310 --> 00:51:47.620
EMILY ROSSER: Yeah,
it's been really fun.

00:51:47.620 --> 00:51:48.880
CARTER CHANG: [INAUDIBLE]?

00:51:48.880 --> 00:51:51.730
EMILY ROSSER: Yeah, the 10
geometers inside that house

00:51:51.730 --> 00:51:52.480
and so it's water.

00:51:52.480 --> 00:51:53.563
CARTER CHANG: It's a seal.

00:51:53.563 --> 00:51:56.892
There's the bag of orange juice.

00:51:56.892 --> 00:51:59.287
Air tight.

00:51:59.287 --> 00:52:01.495
AUDIENCE: Where did
the bags come from?

00:52:01.495 --> 00:52:04.732
CARTER CHANG: So the
plastic bags that we found,

00:52:04.732 --> 00:52:06.190
their similar
thickness to the bags

00:52:06.190 --> 00:52:09.670
that were provided to us by
AsoFenix and by the community

00:52:09.670 --> 00:52:12.220
partner or the
community in El Roblar.

00:52:12.220 --> 00:52:15.100
BRIANNA CONRAD: You can
even purchase long tubes

00:52:15.100 --> 00:52:17.615
and seal up the bottom and
then put the new same seal.

00:52:17.615 --> 00:52:19.740
AUDIENCE: So they would
seal the bottom using that?

00:52:19.740 --> 00:52:21.205
EMILY ROSSER: Yes.

00:52:21.205 --> 00:52:26.120
CARTER CHANG: Or even just sheet
plastic if that's all you had.

00:52:26.120 --> 00:52:29.610
AUDIENCE: And how
many bags do you

00:52:29.610 --> 00:52:32.490
have to go through in
the adjustment process

00:52:32.490 --> 00:52:33.870
to get it down?

00:52:33.870 --> 00:52:36.690
BRIANNA CONRAD: I think I did
about three seals this morning

00:52:36.690 --> 00:52:38.700
to get it set up right.

00:52:38.700 --> 00:52:39.300
And that was--

00:52:39.300 --> 00:52:40.490
AUDIENCE: And you could
do that with water here.

00:52:40.490 --> 00:52:42.365
BRIANNA CONRAD: You
could do that with water.

00:52:42.365 --> 00:52:44.865
I mean, it was this morning,
we started with the temperature

00:52:44.865 --> 00:52:45.630
of the juice.

00:52:45.630 --> 00:52:48.210
We'd like fresh from the store
juice, and it was all cold.

00:52:48.210 --> 00:52:51.600
That added about 5
seconds to the time.

00:52:51.600 --> 00:52:52.710
AUDIENCE: Pre-pressed?

00:52:52.710 --> 00:52:54.345
BRIANNA CONRAD:
Yeah, pre-pressed.

00:52:54.345 --> 00:52:56.400
EMILY ROSSER: So there
are a couple of variables

00:52:56.400 --> 00:52:57.000
you have to juggle.

00:52:57.000 --> 00:52:59.040
There's the thickness of
the plastic, the temperature

00:52:59.040 --> 00:52:59.915
of the liquid inside.

00:52:59.915 --> 00:53:02.460
Also, we found really
pulpy juice kind of

00:53:02.460 --> 00:53:04.440
adds the time as well.

00:53:04.440 --> 00:53:06.480
Hopefully, they wouldn't
waste too much plastic

00:53:06.480 --> 00:53:07.380
in the process.

00:53:07.380 --> 00:53:10.290
What we eventually ended up
doing was of making a seal,

00:53:10.290 --> 00:53:11.250
seeing if it worked.

00:53:11.250 --> 00:53:13.582
If it's nope, pull it down
an inch, make another seal.

00:53:13.582 --> 00:53:14.290
See if it worked.

00:53:14.290 --> 00:53:16.170
So we didn't waste that
much plastic on drill.

00:53:16.170 --> 00:53:18.545
BRIANNA CONRAD: Yeah, it was
like three seals on one bag.

00:53:18.545 --> 00:53:19.640
EMILY ROSSER: On one bag.

00:53:19.640 --> 00:53:22.223
BRIANNA CONRAD: So there was one
bag that was not this pretty,

00:53:22.223 --> 00:53:24.338
but still looked--

00:53:24.338 --> 00:53:26.800
AUDIENCE: How are you
protecting the circuitry?

00:53:26.800 --> 00:53:28.450
CARTER CHANG: So we have it off.

00:53:28.450 --> 00:53:33.010
We used a silicon-base
adhesive around the base,

00:53:33.010 --> 00:53:38.230
and we add that adhesive
around the LEDs and the switch.

00:53:38.230 --> 00:53:39.272
BUS LEONARD: [INAUDIBLE].

00:53:39.272 --> 00:53:41.563
CARTER CHANG: Yeah, PVC pipe
that's been spray painted.

00:53:41.563 --> 00:53:43.870
BRIANNA CONRAD: And
the post clamp and cap.

00:53:43.870 --> 00:53:46.030
We've got at least right
now so we pull it off

00:53:46.030 --> 00:53:47.530
at the [INAUDIBLE]
and take it down.

00:53:47.530 --> 00:53:49.863
CARTER CHANG: And just put a
flat-head screwdriver on it

00:53:49.863 --> 00:53:51.630
and tighten it.

00:53:51.630 --> 00:53:52.970
So it's watertight.

00:53:52.970 --> 00:53:55.370
You can pour water on it.

00:53:55.370 --> 00:53:57.530
EMILY ROSSER: Yeah, we
decided on using PVC

00:53:57.530 --> 00:54:01.670
since that was actually
one of the cheapest

00:54:01.670 --> 00:54:04.910
components we encountered in
hardware stores in Nicaragua.

00:54:04.910 --> 00:54:07.550
It was something like
$0.02 per PVC connectors,

00:54:07.550 --> 00:54:09.740
and I'm sure pipe
is equally cheap.

00:54:09.740 --> 00:54:13.265
So yeah, just keeping
local materials in mind.

00:54:13.265 --> 00:54:14.930
AUDIENCE: And is
the idea they would

00:54:14.930 --> 00:54:17.375
make the circuitry themselves?

00:54:17.375 --> 00:54:20.065
BRIANNA CONRAD: People
probably wouldn't.

00:54:20.065 --> 00:54:21.980
AsoFenix has an
electrical engineer,

00:54:21.980 --> 00:54:26.750
could probably make more or
draw out the best way for it

00:54:26.750 --> 00:54:27.980
to be made in Nicaragua.

00:54:27.980 --> 00:54:30.185
BUS LEONARD: We have a
circuit diagram that we used,

00:54:30.185 --> 00:54:31.670
and that will be sent to them.

00:54:31.670 --> 00:54:34.145
CARTER CHANG: It will be
part of the user's manual.

00:54:34.145 --> 00:54:39.095
AUDIENCE: Is there any safety
on it [INAUDIBLE] burn yourself

00:54:39.095 --> 00:54:42.240
[INAUDIBLE] or does it get hot?

00:54:42.240 --> 00:54:43.732
BUS LEONARD: So I
guess the hope is

00:54:43.732 --> 00:54:46.149
that you wouldn't plug it in
until you're ready to use it.

00:54:46.149 --> 00:54:49.620
And then you would
follow the light cues.

00:54:49.620 --> 00:54:52.116
But I guess if you're
moving it around,

00:54:52.116 --> 00:54:54.476
you can press the button
that's on there it would go on.

00:54:54.476 --> 00:54:56.450
EMILY ROSSER: The nice
thing is that when

00:54:56.450 --> 00:55:00.650
it's pressed in place, it's
pretty much completely sealed.

00:55:00.650 --> 00:55:03.740
And this is Teflon
tape, and the micro wire

00:55:03.740 --> 00:55:05.660
itself pulls off pretty easily.

00:55:05.660 --> 00:55:08.480
Teflon tape doesn't
pull easy at all.

00:55:08.480 --> 00:55:10.975
We've been touching this
all day right after sealing.

00:55:10.975 --> 00:55:12.350
CARTER CHANG: In
other words, you

00:55:12.350 --> 00:55:15.241
can easily shut this down,
hold this with a firm grip

00:55:15.241 --> 00:55:16.866
while it's on and
you still won't feel.

00:55:16.866 --> 00:55:20.080
EMILY ROSSER: [INAUDIBLE].

00:55:20.080 --> 00:55:24.370
BRIANNA CONRAD: If it does
get turned on accidentally,

00:55:24.370 --> 00:55:27.174
if you were touching it, or
it got turned on [INAUDIBLE]..

00:55:32.460 --> 00:55:34.634
It doesn't turn on the
[INAUDIBLE] or anything.

00:55:38.480 --> 00:55:40.250
if the arm is open
for it to not turn on.

00:55:40.250 --> 00:55:41.833
AUDIENCE: If they
wanted to connect it

00:55:41.833 --> 00:55:43.895
to a 12-volt-DC battery,
what would they do?

00:55:43.895 --> 00:55:47.810
BRIANNA CONRAD: We've got
these today for simplicity,

00:55:47.810 --> 00:55:50.900
strung together, but you'd
just take these two leads,

00:55:50.900 --> 00:55:52.970
and instead of
splattering, attaching them

00:55:52.970 --> 00:55:55.640
to wires that are
in the adapter,

00:55:55.640 --> 00:55:58.330
attach them tip
clips to a battery.

